hadiths[685]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6210","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه said: It was asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, which earnings are purest?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The work of a man with his own hand, and every righteous, honest sale.""**"
"6211","Jumayʿ ibn ʿUmayr reported from his maternal uncle, Abū Burdah ibn Niyār رضي الله عنه: The Prophet ﷺ was asked about the most virtuous earnings, and he said: > **""An honest, righteous sale, and the work of a man with his own hand.""**"
"6212","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked, ""Which earnings are most virtuous?"" He said: > **""The work of a man with his own hand, and every righteous, honest sale.""**"
"6213","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The best earnings are the earnings of the working hand, when he is sincere and faithful.""**"
"6214","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6215","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6216","It is reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Blessed has been granted for my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6217","ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6218","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours on its Thursday.""**"
"6219","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما then advised: do not ask a man for a need at night, and do not ask a blind man for a need, for modesty resides in the eyes. > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours on its Thursday.""**"
"6220","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Go out early in seeking provision, for going out in the morning is blessing and success.""**"
"6221","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours, and place it on Thursday.""**"
"6222","Nubayṭ ibn Sharīṭ رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours on its Thursday.""**"
"6223","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6224","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched a military expedition, he would send it out at the beginning of the day, in the morning, and he would pray: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6225","Jābir رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6226","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6227","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6228","Kaʿb ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6229","Al-Nawwās ibn Samʿān al-Kilābī رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, bless my Ummah in its early morning hours.""**"
"6230","ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Sleeping in the morning withholds provision.""**"
"6231","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah loves the believing craftsman, skilled in his profession.""**"
"6232","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها said: Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه set out in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as a merchant to Buṣrā. Neither his attachment to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his eagerness for his company prevented him from journeying for trade — for they admired earning through trade and loved trade — nor did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prevent Abū Bakr from journeying for his trade out of love for his companionship and attachment to him, for he admired his companionship, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ considered trade good and admired it."
"6233","Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah رضي الله عنه said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when ʿUrfuṭah ibn Nuhayk al-Tamīmī stood and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I and my household earn our provision from this hunting. It is our portion and our blessing, yet it preoccupies us from the remembrance of Allah and from prayer in congregation, and we are in need of it. Do you declare it lawful or unlawful?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""I declare it lawful, for Allah Almighty has declared it lawful. It is an excellent occupation, and Allah is most worthy of accepting excuses. Before me there were Messengers of Allah, all of whom hunted and pursued game. Sufficient for you regarding congregational prayer, when you are absent from it seeking provision, is your love for the congregation and its people, and your love for the remembrance of Allah and its people. Seek the lawful for yourself and your dependents, for that is striving in the path of Allah Almighty. And know that the help of Allah lies in righteous trade.""**"
"6234","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If Allah were to permit trade to the people of Paradise, they would trade in cloth and perfume.""**"
"6235","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: I dislike seeing a man idle, engaged in neither the work of this world nor the work of the Hereafter."
"6236","Anas رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: It is explained that what is meant by the establishment of the Hour is its signs, for it has also been narrated: when one of you hears of the Dajjāl while a palm-shoot is in his hand, let him plant it, for people will still have livelihood after it. > **""If the Hour is established while a palm-shoot is in the hand of one of you, let him plant it.""**"
"6237","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seek provision in the hidden depths of the earth.""**"
"6238","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever enters the evening weary from the work of his hands enters the evening forgiven.""**"
"6239","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Among sins are sins which neither prayer, nor fasting, nor Hajj, nor ʿUmrah expiate."" They asked, ""What then expiates them, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: > **""Anxieties and toils in seeking a livelihood.""**"
"6240","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to trade to al-Shām by sea. In another wording: they used to trade in the Sanctuary."
"6241","Al-Ḥasan said: ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ carried some people on a sea voyage, and news of that reached ʿUmar. ʿUmar said: he carried people with nothing between them and the sea except planks. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if they had perished, I would have taken their blood-money from Thaqīf."
"6242","ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for me and said, ""Take your garments and your weapons, then come to me."" So I came to him while he was performing ablution. He looked me up and down, then lowered his gaze and said, ""I intend to send you at the head of an army; Allah will keep you safe and grant you spoils, and I desire for you a good portion of wealth."" I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I did not accept Islam for the sake of wealth; rather I accepted Islam out of desire for Islam and to be with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" He said: > **""O ʿAmr, how excellent is righteous wealth for the righteous man.""**"
"6243","Ḥabīb ibn ʿUbayd said: Al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib رضي الله عنه had a slave girl who sold milk, and al-Miqdām himself collected the price. It was said to him, ""Glory be to Allah! She sells the milk and you take the price?"" He replied, ""Yes, and there is no harm in that. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" > **""There will surely come upon people a time in which nothing will benefit except the dinar and the dirham.""**"
"6244","Al-Miqdām رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There will come upon people a time when whoever has no gold and no silver will not enjoy a wholesome life.""**"
"6245","Ḥabīb ibn ʿUbayd said: I saw al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib رضي الله عنه in the marketplace, his slave girl selling milk while he sat collecting the dirhams. When questioned about that, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""When the end of time comes, people will inevitably need dirhams and dinars, by which a man upholds his religion and his worldly life.""**"
"6246","Jarīr رضي الله عنه said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw that I kept nothing and spent everything I had, he said: > **""O Jarīr, there is no blame on you for holding back your wealth, for this matter has a duration.""**"
"6247","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Dinars and dirhams are the seals of Allah upon His earth; whoever comes with the seal of his Master, his need is fulfilled.""**"
"6248","Zayd ibn Aslam reported from a man of Banū Sulaym, from his grandfather: He came to the Prophet ﷺ with silver, saying, ""This is from a mine of ours."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There will be mines which the worst of people will attend.""**"
"6249","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه said: The owners of camels and the owners of sheep boasted before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pride and arrogance are among the owners of camels, while calmness and dignity are among the owners of sheep.""** > **""Mūsā عليه السلام was sent while tending sheep for his family, and I was sent while tending sheep for my family at Jiyād.""**"
"6250","Wahb ibn Kaysān said: My father passed by Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, who asked, ""Where are you headed?"" He replied, ""To some sheep of mine."" He said, ""Excellent. Wipe their dust from their noses, keep their resting place clean, pray beside their resting place, and be fond of them, for they are among the beasts of Paradise. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" > **""It is a land of scant rainfall,"" meaning Madinah.**"
"6251","Umm Hāniʾ رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her: > **""Keep sheep, O Umm Hāniʾ, for they go out in the morning with good and return in the evening with good.""**"
"6252","Umm Hāniʾ رضي الله عنها said: The Prophet ﷺ entered and asked, ""Why do I see nothing of the blessings with you?"" I asked, ""Which blessings do you mean?"" He said: > **""Indeed Allah Almighty has sent down three blessings: the sheep, the palm tree, and fire.""**"
"6253","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Honor the goats, and wipe the dust from their noses, for they are among the beasts of Paradise.""**"
"6254","Saʿīd reported from Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do good to goats, and remove harm from them, for they are among the beasts of Paradise.""**"
"6255","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Calmness is among the owners of sheep and cattle.""**"
"6256","Ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah reported from ʿAlī رضي الله عنه, in a narration raised to the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: > **""There are no people with a sheep in their house — meaning a milking sheep — except that they are sanctified twice each day, or blessed upon twice.""**"
"6257","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Treat goats with kindness, for they are delicate wealth and they are in Paradise. The most beloved wealth to Allah is sheep. Wear white, for Allah created Paradise white; let your living wear it and shroud your dead in it. And the blood of a white sheep is greater with Allah than the blood of two black ones.""**"
"6258","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""How pious he is! How pious he is! How pious he is — a shepherd of sheep upon a mountain peak, establishing the prayer therein.""**"
"6259","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When asked what ""rughām"" meant, he explained: nasal mucus. > **""Keep to sheep, for they are among the beasts of Paradise. Pray in their resting place and wipe their noses.""**"
"6260","ʿAbdullāh ibn Sāʿidah, the brother of ʿUwaym ibn Sāʿidah رضي الله عنهما, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever owns sheep, let him go out with them away from Madinah, for Madinah is the land of Allah with the least rainfall.""**"
"6261","Al-Barāʾ رضي الله عنه said: Sheep are blessing."
"6262","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ complaining of loneliness and gloom, so he commanded him to keep a pair of pigeons."
"6263","Abū Kabshah al-Anmārī رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ delighted in gazing at the citron, and he delighted in gazing at the red pigeon."
"6264","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: I leave behind me nothing dearer to me than camels and young irrigated palms."
"6265","Khallād ibn al-Sāʾib reported from his father رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever sows a crop, and birds or any seekers of provision eat from it, it is charity for him.""**"
"6266","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no man who plants a planting except that Allah writes for him reward equal to what comes forth from its fruit.""**"
"6267","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه was once seen planting a sapling in Damascus. A man passing by said to him, ""Do you do this while you are a Companion of the Messenger of Allah?"" He replied, ""Do not hasten against me. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" > **""Whoever plants a planting, no human nor any creature of Allah eats from it except that it is charity for him.""**"
"6268","Fannaj said: I used to work in Daynābādh tending the land when Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah arrived as governor of Yemen, accompanied by men from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. One of those who came with him found me in the crop, diverting water among it, with walnuts in his sleeve. He sat by a water-channel, cracking and eating from those walnuts, then motioned to me, ""Come here, O Persian."" So I drew near. He asked, ""Will you guarantee the planting of these walnuts upon this water?"" Fannaj replied, ""What benefit is in that for me?"" The man said, ""I heard with these two ears of mine the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" Fannaj asked, ""Did you yourself hear this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" He said yes. Fannaj said, ""Then I guarantee it."" And from that came the walnuts of Daynābādh. > **""Whoever sets up a tree, then perseveres in guarding it and tending it until it bears fruit, he shall have charity with Allah Almighty for everything taken from its fruit.""**"
"6269","Al-Sāʾib ibn Suwayd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is nothing taken from the crop of any one of you by seekers of provision except that Allah writes reward for him thereby.""**"
"6270","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Those firmly rooted in mud and eaten in times of dearth — meaning date-palms — whoever sells them, the price thereof is like ashes upon a lofty peak which the wind scatters.""**"
"6271","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about date-palms, and he said: > **""Those are firmly rooted in mud and eaten in times of dearth.""**"
"6272","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Date-palms and trees are a blessing for their owners, and for their descendants after them, when they are grateful to Allah.""**"
"6273","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said: The Prophet ﷺ commanded his uncle al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه to order his sons to plow fresh forage — al-qaḍb meaning moist herbage — for it removes poverty."
"6274","Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥaydah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said, conveying from Allah and not from the Messenger himself: > **""May Allah curse the cutter of the lote tree.""**"
"6275","ʿAmr ibn Aws رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever cuts the lote tree, except what is removed for cultivation, Allah will build for him a house in the Fire.""**"
"6276","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥubshī رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: meaning the lote trees of the Sanctuary. > **""Whoever cuts a lote tree, Allah will plunge his head into the Fire,""**"
"6277","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: The Prophet ﷺ fixed the protected boundary of a date-palm as the reach of its branches."
"6278","Jābir رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When Allah intends evil for a servant, He makes bricks and clay alluring to him, until he builds.""**"
"6279","Abū Bashīr al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When Allah intends humiliation for His servant, He causes him to spend his wealth on buildings.""**"
"6280","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by the dome-like structure of a man from the Anṣār and asked, ""What is this?"" They said, ""A dome."" The Prophet ﷺ said, pointing with his hand above his head: > **""Every building greater than this is a burden upon its owner on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6281","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds beyond what suffices him will be burdened with carrying it upon his neck on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6282","Abū al-ʿĀliyah said: Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضي الله عنه built an upper chamber, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him: He asked, ""Shall I demolish it or give its price in charity?"" He said: > **""Demolish it.""** > **""Demolish it.""**"
"6283","Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنهما said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbidding riding upon the skins of predators and forbidding the raising of buildings high."
"6284","Muʿādh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a building without injustice or transgression, or plants a planting without injustice or transgression, he shall have ongoing reward for as long as any creature of the Most Merciful, Blessed and Exalted, benefits thereby.""**"
"6285","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew a parable for provision: > **""The likeness of provision is that of a wall with a door. Around the door is ease, and around the wall is roughness and hardship. Whoever approaches it from its door attains it fully and safely, and whoever approaches it from the side of the wall falls into roughness and hardship until, when he reaches it, he gets nothing but the provision which Allah Almighty made easy for him.""**"
"6286","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O people, richness is not from abundance of goods, but richness is richness of the soul. Allah Almighty will fulfill for His servant what He has written for him of provision, so be graceful in seeking. Take what is lawful and leave what He has forbidden.""**"
"6287","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ stood, called the people, and they came and sat before him. He then informed them of what Jibrīl عليه السلام had conveyed to him: > **""Come to me. This is the messenger of the Lord of the worlds, Jibrīl. He breathed into my heart that no soul will die until it completes its provision, even if it is delayed for it. So fear Allah and be graceful in seeking, and let not delay in provision drive you to seek it through disobedience of Allah Almighty, for what is with Allah is attained only through obedience to Him.""**"
"6288","ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAdī رضي الله عنه said: He had purchased one hundred shares from the shares of Khaybar. When that reached the Prophet ﷺ, he said: > **""Two hungry wolves let loose among sheep neglected by their owner are not more harmful than a Muslim's pursuit of wealth and status to his religion.""**"
"6289","Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not hasten toward a thing thinking that if you hasten to it you will attain it, if Allah did not decree it for you. And do not hold back from a thing thinking that if you hold back from it it will be averted from you, if Allah has decreed it upon you.""**"
"6290","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a fallen date, took it, and handed it to a beggar. He then said: > **""Had you not come to it, it would have come to you.""**"
"6291","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Do not please anyone through the displeasure of Allah, nor praise anyone for the bounty of Allah, nor blame anyone for what Allah has not given you. For the provision of Allah is not driven to you by the greed of the greedy, nor averted from you by the hatred of the hater. Allah, in His justice and fairness, has placed rest and joy in contentment and certainty, and has placed worry and sorrow in discontent.""**"
"6292","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended the minbar on the day of the expedition of Tabūk, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: > **""O people, by Allah, I command you only with what Allah has commanded you, and I forbid you only from what Allah has forbidden you. So be graceful in seeking. By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Abū al-Qāsim, the provision of each one of you seeks him as his death seeks him. If anything of it is difficult for you, then seek it through obedience of Allah Almighty.""**"
"6293","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Holy Spirit breathed into my heart that no soul will leave this world until it completes its term and exhausts its provision. So be graceful in seeking, and let not delay in provision drive you to seek it through disobedience of Allah, for what is with Allah is attained only through obedience to Him.""**"
"6294","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no morning that Allah creates but that provision belongs to the servant. Even if both heavy hosts, jinn and men, gathered to withhold anything of it from him, they could not.""**"
"6295","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: In another wording: > **""Provision surely seeks the servant as his death seeks him.""** > **""More than his death seeks him.""**"
"6296","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه said: He heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Provision is neither diminished by disobedience nor increased by a good deed, and abandoning supplication is disobedience.""**"
"6297","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If one of you were to flee from his provision, it would catch up with him as death catches up with him.""**"
"6298","Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The lands are the lands of Allah and the servants are the servants of Allah, so wherever you find good, settle there.""**"
"6299","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to a group of traders and called out to them. They responded and craned their necks toward him. He said: > **""O company of traders, Allah will raise you on the Day of Resurrection as wicked, except one who is truthful, righteous, and fulfills the trust.""**"
"6300","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no good in trade except for one who does not overpraise his sale, does not disparage what he buys, earns lawfully and gives lawfully, and avoids oaths therein.""**"
"6301","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would come out to us while we were traders, and he would say: > **""O company of traders, beware of lying.""**"
"6302","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shibl al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, did Allah not permit sale?"" He said: > **""The traders are the wicked, the traders are the wicked.""** > **""Yes, but they speak and lie, and they swear oaths and sin.""**"
"6303","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shibl رضي الله عنه said: He heard the Prophet ﷺ say: And he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: They said, ""O Messenger of Allah, did Allah not permit sale?"" He said: And he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: They said, ""O Messenger of Allah, who are the defiantly disobedient?"" He said: A man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, are they not our mothers, our sisters, and our wives?"" He said: > **""Recite the Qurʾān, do not go to excess in it, do not turn away from it, do not eat by it, and do not seek abundance through it.""** > **""The traders are the wicked.""** > **""Yes, but they speak and lie, and they swear oaths and sin.""** > **""The defiantly disobedient are the people of the Fire.""** > **""Women.""** > **""Yes, but when they are given they are not grateful, and when they are tested they are not patient.""**"
"6304","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: In the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we did not kill the traders of the polytheists."
"6305","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters. Whoever avoids them is more protective of his religion and his honor, and whoever falls into them is close to falling into major sins, like one grazing beside a sanctuary, close to entering it. Every king has a sanctuary, and the sanctuary of Allah is His bounds.""**"
"6306","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters. Whoever avoids them is more innocent in his religion and his honor, and whoever falls into doubtful matters is close to falling into the unlawful, like one grazing around a sanctuary, close to entering it without realizing.""**"
"6307","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, so leave what makes you doubt for what does not make you doubt.""**"
"6308","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Part of a man's understanding is his gentleness in his livelihood.""**"
"6309","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: He heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Gentleness in livelihood is better than some trade.""**"
"6310","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Be lenient and you will be treated leniently.""**"
"6311","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A man entered Paradise through his leniency in paying and in demanding payment.""**"
"6312","A man from Balʿadawiyyah reported that his grandfather said: He set out for Madinah and alighted by the valley, where two men were disputing over a single goat, and the buyer was saying to the seller, ""Deal with me kindly."" He wondered whether this was the Hāshimī man. Then he saw a man of handsome build, broad forehead, fine nose, thin eyebrows, with a line of black hair like a black thread from the hollow of his throat to his navel, wearing two worn garments. He came near and greeted: They returned the greeting. Soon the buyer called out, ""O Messenger of Allah, tell him to deal kindly with me."" He extended his hand and said: Then he went on. > **""Peace be upon you.""** > **""You own your wealth. I hope to meet Allah Almighty on the Day of Resurrection with none of you demanding of me anything I wronged him in of wealth, blood, or honor, except by its right. May Allah have mercy on a man who is easy in selling, easy in buying, easy in taking, easy in giving, easy in paying, and easy in demanding.""**"
"6313","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The most virtuous of the believers is a man who is lenient in selling, lenient in buying, lenient in paying, and lenient in demanding.""**"
"6314","Muʿayqīb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Fire has been forbidden upon the gentle, the soft, the easy, the approachable.""**"
"6315","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: In another wording: > **""Shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise? Every gentle, soft, easy, approachable person.""** > **""Shall I not inform you upon whom the Fire is forbidden?""**"
"6316","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The Fire is forbidden upon every gentle, soft, easy, approachable person.""**"
"6317","Anas رضي الله عنه said: It was asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, who is forbidden upon the Fire?"" He said: > **""The gentle, the soft, the easy, the approachable.""**"
"6318","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: A man complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of poor skill in his craft. He said: When asked about it, he explained it as a foal or a boy, meaning train them young. > **""Raise a small one.""**"
"6319","Ghassān ibn al-Agharr al-Nahshalī reported from his father, from his grandfather: He came with his camel to Madinah carrying food, and the Prophet ﷺ met him and asked: He said, ""I have prepared wheat."" He asked: He said, ""I want to sell it."" So he wiped his head and said to the people: > **""O Bedouin, what are you carrying?""** > **""What do you want?""** > **""Deal well with the Bedouin in bargaining.""**"
"6320","Ghassān ibn al-Agharr al-Nahshalī reported through his uncle Ziyād ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, from his father Ḥuṣayn ibn Qays, that he carried food to Madinah, and the same account as above was mentioned, in which the Prophet ﷺ enjoined good bargaining with the Bedouin."
"6321","Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما reported, raising it to the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: > **""The cheated one is neither praised nor rewarded.""**"
"6322","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The cheated one is neither praised nor rewarded.""**"
"6323","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said: He heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Cheating one who trusts you is unlawful.""**"
"6324","Abū Usayd رضي الله عنه reported: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""May my father and mother be ransomed for you, I have seen a place for a market, will you not look at it?"" He said: So he stood with him until he came to the site of the market. When he saw it, it pleased him, and he struck it with his foot and said: > **""Yes.""** > **""What a good market of yours this is. Let it not be broken up, and let no tax be levied upon it.""**"
"6325","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه reported: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, which lands are worst?"" He said: When Jibrīl عليه السلام came to him, he asked him, and Jibrīl said he did not know until he asked his Lord Almighty. Jibrīl went away, remained as long as Allah willed, then came and said, ""O Muḥammad, you asked me which lands are worst, and I said I did not know. I asked my Lord Almighty which lands are worst, and He said:"" > **""I do not know.""** > **""Their markets.""**"
"6326","Jubayr رضي الله عنه reported in the narration of al-Bazzār: A man asked which lands are most beloved to Allah and which lands are most hateful to Allah. The Prophet ﷺ said: When he came, he informed him: > **""I do not know until I ask Jibrīl.""** > **""The most beloved places to Allah are the mosques, and the most hateful places to Allah are the markets.""**"
"6327","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked Jibrīl: He said, ""I do not know."" He said: So Jibrīl عليه السلام wept and said, ""O Muḥammad, are we to ask Him? He informs us of what He wills."" He ascended to the heaven, then came back and said, ""The best of places are the houses of Allah on earth."" He asked: So he ascended to the heaven, then came back and said: > **""Which places are best?""** > **""Then ask your Lord Almighty about that.""** > **""Then which places are worst?""** > **""The worst of places are the markets.""**"
"6328","Salmān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: In another narration: Or he said: > **""Do not be the first to enter the market, nor the last to leave it, for therein Satan lays his eggs and hatches them.""** > **""For it is a battlefield.""** > **""It is the resting place of Satan, and therein he pitches his banner.""**"
"6329","Salmān رضي الله عنه said: He heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever goes early to the dawn prayer is given a quarter of faith, and whoever goes early to the market is given the banner of Iblīs, which stays with the first to go early and the last to return.""**"
"6330","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said: He heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The devils go early with their banners to the markets, entering with the first to enter and leaving with the last to leave.""**"
"6331","Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه went out to the market, where a man was saying, ""People fought in the market; I have never seen a misleading trial like today."" Ibn Masʿūd said, ""This is not the misleading trial, but this is the horn of Satan."""
"6332","Bilād ibn ʿIṣmah said that while he was walking with ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه, he saw a crowd and went toward it, then returned. ʿAbdullāh said to him, ""Beware the throng of the market, for it is the throng of Satan."""
"6333","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the market, he would say: > **""O Allah, I ask You for the good of this market and the good of what is in it, and I seek refuge in You from its evil and the evil of what is in it. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from taking a wicked oath therein or making a losing bargain.""**"
"6334","In another narration, the Prophet ﷺ would say: > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of this market, and I seek refuge in You from disbelief and wickedness.""**"
"6335","Salmān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: In another wording: > **""Three whom Allah will not look at on the Day of Resurrection: an old man who commits adultery, a poor man who is arrogant, and a man who has made Allah his merchandise—he buys only with an oath and sells only with an oath.""** > **""Three to whom Allah will not speak, whom He will not purify, and for whom is a painful punishment.""**"
"6336","ʿIṣmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three whom Allah will not look at tomorrow: an old man who commits adultery, a man who takes oaths as his merchandise in every truth and falsehood, and a poor man who is proud and self-admiring.""**"
"6337","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The measure is the measure of the people of Makkah, and the scale is the scale of the people of Madinah.""**"
"6338","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by food whose owner had beautified it. He put his hand into it and found bad food beneath. He said: > **""Sell this separately and this separately. Whoever cheats us is not of us.""**"
"6339","Abū Burdah ibn Niyār رضي الله عنه said: They set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to Baqīʿ al-Muṣallā. He put his hand into food, then took it out, and it was adulterated or mixed. He said: > **""He who cheats us is not of us.""**"
"6340","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever cheats us is not of us.""**"
"6341","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever cheats us is not of us, and deceit and trickery are in the Fire.""**"
"6342","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever cheats us is not of us.""**"
"6343","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه said: He set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to the market of al-Naqīʿ. He put his hand into a heap and brought out different food—or adulterated food. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""He who cheats us is not of us.""**"
"6344","Qays ibn Abī Gharazah رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ passed by a man selling food and asked: He said, ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O owner of the food, is the bottom of it like the top of it?""** > **""Whoever cheats the Muslims is not of them.""**"
"6345","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever cheats us is not of us, and whoever shoots arrows at us is not of us.""**"
"6346","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ passed by, put his hand into the food, and said: > **""Whoever cheats us is not of us.""**"
"6347","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever cheats us is not of us.""**"
"6348","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the market and saw piled-up food. He put his hand into it and found wet food that rain had struck. He asked its owner: He said, ""By Him Who sent you with the truth, it is one food."" He said: > **""What drove you to this?""** > **""Why did you not separate the wet on its own and the dry on its own, so that they buy knowingly? Whoever cheats us is not of us.""**"
"6349","One of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reported: The Prophet ﷺ intended to forbid sale, so they said, ""O Messenger of Allah, it is our livelihood."" He said: > **""Then let there be no deceit.""**"
"6350","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim. It is not lawful for a Muslim to conceal what is in his goods from his brother—if he knew of it, he would leave it.""**"
"6351","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you sells goods, let him not conceal a defect if there is one in them.""**"
"6352","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: Meaning the runaway camel. > **""The runaway is to be returned.""**"
"6353","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not buy fish in the water, for it is deception.""**"
"6354","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the deceptive sale."
"6355","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the deceptive sale."
"6356","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the deceptive sale."
"6357","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade shighār, the sale of majar, the deceptive sale, the sale of debt for debt, and the sale of deferred payment for prompt payment. Majar is what is in the wombs. Gharar is to sell what you do not have. Debt for debt is debt in exchange for debt. Deferred for prompt is when you are owed a thousand dirhams by a man and he says, ""I will pay you five hundred promptly and you leave the rest."" Shighār is to marry a woman in exchange for a woman with no dowry between them."
"6358","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not engage in artificial bidding, do not engage in mulāmasah, do not trade in deception, let no city-dweller sell for a Bedouin, and whoever buys a muḥaffalah ewe, let him milk it for three days—then if he returns it, let him return it with a ṣāʿ of dates.""**"
"6359","Zāmil ibn ʿAmr reported from his father, from his grandfather: The Prophet ﷺ went out on the day of Fiṭr to the ʿĪd, with Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه on his right and ʿUmar رضي الله عنه on his left. When he finished, he passed by the gate of Abū Kathīr where the butchers were in its courtyard, while the people were fresh from ignorance. He asked: They said, ""For such-and-such."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""How do you sell?""** > **""Sell as you wish, but do not mix carrion with slaughtered meat for the people. O people, take heed: do not hoard, do not engage in artificial bidding, do not meet goods before the market, let no city-dweller sell for a Bedouin, let no man sell over his brother's sale, let no man propose over his brother's proposal until he permits him, and let no woman ask for the divorce of another so as to overturn her vessel and be married instead, for her provision is upon Allah Almighty.""**"
"6360","Abu al-Darda' (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed on a day of Fitr or Adha, then turned away, followed by Ubayy, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, Abdullah ibn Amr, and himself, until they reached the butchers near the house of Abu Kathir, where he ﷺ addressed them. > **""Do not skin your slaughtered animal until it dies, and let none of you sell over the sale of another, and do not practise najsh, and do not forestall goods, and do not hoard.""**"
"6361","Abu Umamah al-Bahili (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the people of cities who are confined in the path of Allah as the support and frontier of the Muslims. > **""The people of the cities are confined in the path of Allah, the support of the Muslims and their frontier, so do not overprice against them, and do not hoard, and let no townsman sell for a Bedouin, and let no man bid over the bidding of his brother, nor propose over his proposal, nor let a woman overturn the vessel of her sister; and the provision of each is upon Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"6362","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a woman being married on condition of another's divorce, a man selling over his companion's sale before he abandons it, three men in a desert land being without an appointed leader, and two of three in a desert whispering apart from their companion. > **""It is not lawful for a woman to be married upon the divorce of another, nor is it lawful for a man to sell over the sale of his companion until he leaves it, nor is it lawful for three persons in a desert land except that they appoint one of them over them, nor is it lawful for three persons in a desert land that two of them whisper apart from their companion.""**"
"6363","Imran ibn Husayn (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade jalab and janab, forbade mulamasah, forbade najsh along with sale, and forbade a man buying over his brother's purchase or proposing over his brother's proposal. > **""There is no jalab nor janab. One does not practise mulamasah, nor najsh in sale, nor does a man buy over the purchase of his brother or propose over the proposal of his brother.""**"
"6364","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Prophet ﷺ a comprehensive prohibition covering sale for the Bedouin, meeting caravans, najsh, proposing over one's brother, a woman seeking her sister's divorce to overturn her dish, and tasriyah of camels and sheep for sale. > **""Let no townsman sell for a Bedouin, and do not meet the caravans, and do not practise najsh, and let none of you propose over the proposal of his brother, and let no woman ask for the divorce of her sister to overturn what is in her dish, for hers is only what is written; and do not leave camels and sheep unmilked for sale. Whoever buys a musarrah sheep has one of two views: if he returns it, he returns it with a sa' of dates.""**"
"6365","Samurah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ forbade meeting incoming goods before they reach the markets, and forbade a townsman selling for a Bedouin. > **""Incoming goods are not to be met until they reach the markets, nor is a townsman to sell for a Bedouin.""**"
"6366","In an additional wording the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say, forbidding the meeting of caravans before their market and sale on behalf of nomads without exception for kinship. > **""Do not meet the caravans until they reach their market, and do not sell for the nomads, even if he be the brother of one of you, or his father, or his mother.""**"
"6367","A Companion of the Prophet ﷺ reported from the Prophet ﷺ forbidding the meeting of goods, sale of a townsman for a Bedouin, and defining the buyer's option in a musarrah sheep or she-camel after milking, with a sa' of food or dates in return. > **""Goods are not to be met, nor is a townsman to sell for a Bedouin. Whoever buys a musarrah sheep or she-camel has the last of the two views when he milks it: if he returns it, he returns with it a sa' of food.""**"
"6368","Amr ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ a concise prohibition of forestalling and of town for desert agency. > **""Do not meet the caravans, and let no townsman sell for a Bedouin.""**"
"6369","Hakim ibn Abi Yazid reported from his father, who narrated from his father, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded leaving people to benefit from one another, with sincere counsel when sought. > **""Leave people, that some of them may obtain from others; and when one of you seeks sincere advice from his brother, let him give him sincere counsel.""**"
"6370","Hakim ibn Abi Yazid reported from his father, from one who heard the Prophet ﷺ, the same command to leave people to obtain provision from one another. > **""Leave people, that some of them may obtain from others; and when one of you seeks sincere advice from his brother, let him give him sincere counsel.""**"
"6371","Hakim ibn Abi Yazid reported from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, commanding non-interference in mutual provision and sincerity when consulted. > **""Leave people, that some of them may be sustained through others; and when one of you seeks sincere advice from his brother, let him advise him.""**"
"6372","Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib reported from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded leaving people to obtain from one another and advising a brother who seeks counsel. > **""Leave people, that some of them may obtain from others; and when your brother seeks your sincere counsel, give him sincere counsel.""**"
"6373","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade town agency for the desert in both directions. > **""Let no townsman sell for a Bedouin, nor buy for him.""**"
"6374","Nu'aym ibn Husayn al-Sadusi reported from his uncle, from his grandfather, that he came to Medina with camels of his while the Prophet ﷺ was there, asked him ﷺ to instruct the marketplace people to deal fairly with him and aid him, so they stood with him until he sold his camels; then the Prophet ﷺ drew him near, wiped his hand over his forelock, and supplicated for him three times. > **""Come near.""**"
"6375","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the najish, who inflates prices without intent to buy. > **""The najish is a consumer of riba, accursed.""**"
"6376","Ismah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ negated private enclosure and false bidding in Islam. > **""There is no hima in Islam, nor munajashah.""**"
"6377","Samurah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade encroaching upon a brother's proposal or transaction. > **""A man is not to propose over the proposal of his brother, nor buy over his purchase.""**"
"6378","Zayd ibn Aslam reported that he heard a man ask Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) about auction sale, muzayadah, and Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) answered with the Prophetic prohibition, excepting spoils and inheritances. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade that one of you sell over the sale of his brother, except spoils and inheritances.""**"
"6379","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade encroachment in sale and marriage. > **""Let none of you buy over the purchase of his brother, nor propose over the proposal of his brother.""**"
"6380","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported from a man of the Ansar who complained of need to the Prophet ﷺ. When asked what he possessed, he brought a coarse cloth and a cup. The Prophet ﷺ asked who would buy them; a man offered a dirham, so he ﷺ asked who would increase, the people fell silent, then a man offered two dirhams, so he ﷺ awarded them to him, then defined lawful begging. > **""Who will buy this? Who will increase over a dirham? Who will increase? They are yours. Begging is not lawful except for one of three: painful blood-money, crushing debt, or grinding poverty.""**"
"6381","Sufyan ibn Wahb (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ forbid muzayadah, that is, competitive increase in bidding. > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade muzayadah.""**"
"6382","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade two transactions in one. Simak, a narrator, explained it as a seller saying: it is for such-and-such on credit, and for such-and-such in cash. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade two transactions in one sale.""**"
"6383","In the wording of al-Tabarani in al-Awsat from Ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه), the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared two transactions in one unlawful. > **""Two transactions in one are not lawful.""**"
"6384","Ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) is reported in al-Kabir, as a stopped narration, that a single concluded bargain set against two stipulated bargains is riba; in al-Bazzar's wording with the addition of the command to perfect ablution."
"6385","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the solvent debtor's delay as injustice, commanded following a transfer to a solvent party, and forbade two sales in one. > **""The delay of the rich is injustice; and when one of you is transferred to a solvent man, let him follow him; and no two sales in one.""**"
"6386","Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id reported that he came to Mecca and found Abu Hanifah, Ibn Abi Layla, and Ibn Shubrumah, and asked each about a man who sells with a stipulated condition: the first said both sale and condition are void, the second said the sale is valid and the condition void, the third said both are valid. When each was informed of the others' disagreement, Abu Hanifah cited through Amr ibn Shu'ayb from his father from his grandfather the Prophetic prohibition of a sale with a condition, Ibn Abi Layla cited through Hisham ibn Urwah from his father from A'ishah (رضي الله عنها) the command to buy Barirah and free her, and Ibn Shubrumah cited through Mis'ar ibn Kidam from Muharib ibn Dithar from Jabir ibn Abdillah (رضي الله عنهما) the Prophet's ﷺ sale of a she-camel with stipulated riding to Medina."
"6387","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to Attab ibn Asid (رضي الله عنه) when sending him to the people of Allah, the people of Mecca, commanding him to forbid them certain transactions. > **""I have sent you to the people of Allah, the people of Mecca, so forbid them sale of what is not possessed, and profit of what is not guaranteed, and two conditions within a condition, and sale with a loan, and sale with salaf.""**"
"6388","Hakim ibn Hizam (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade him four qualities in sale: salaf with sale, two conditions in a sale, selling what one does not have, and profit of what is not guaranteed. > **""Salaf with sale, two conditions in a sale, sale of what is not with you, and profit of what is not guaranteed.""**"
"6389","Attab ibn Asid (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ, when appointing him over Mecca, asked whether he would convey from him what he commanded, namely to tell his people not to combine sale with salaf, not to sell gharar sales, and not to sell what is not with them. > **""Will you convey from me to your people what I command you with? Let none of you combine a sale with salaf, nor sell a gharar sale, nor sell what is not with him.""**"
"6390","Abdullah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade two fasts, two prayers, two garments, two foods, two marriages, and two sales: fasting the days of Fitr and Adha; prayer after dawn until sunrise and after Asr until sunset; wrapping in one garment with nothing between one's nakedness and the sky; eating with the left while the right is sound and eating reclined; a man saying sell to me and I shall sell to you; and marriage of the adulteress and marriage over the maternal or paternal aunt. > **""Two fasts: the day of Fitr and the day of Adha. Two prayers: prayer after dawn until the sun rises, and prayer after Asr until the sun sets. Two garments: wrapping in one garment with nothing between his nakedness and the sky. Two foods: eating with his left while his right is sound, and eating reclined. Two sales: a man saying, sell to me and I shall sell to you. Two marriages: marriage of the adulteress, and marriage over the maternal aunt and paternal aunt.""**"
"6391","Ma'qil ibn Yasar (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade stipulating emancipation upon the sellers when buying a neck in order to free it, for it is a bond of provision. > **""Whoever buys a neck to free it, let emancipation not be stipulated upon its owners; for it is a bond of provision.""**"
"6392","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed return of a temporary grant and adherence to stipulations in accord with truth. > **""The grant is to be returned, and people are upon their conditions so long as they accord with the truth.""**"
"6393","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ declared void every condition lacking sanction in Allah's Book, even a hundred conditions. > **""Every condition not in the Book of Allah is void, even if a hundred conditions.""**"
"6394","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported in another wording that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed wala for the emancipator, then asked what is with people who stipulate conditions not in Allah's Book, declaring every such condition rejected. > **""Wala belongs to the one who frees. What is with people who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah? Whatever condition is not in the Book of Allah is rejected.""**"
"6395","Imran ibn Husayn (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade selling weapons during civil strife. > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade selling arms in fitnah.""**"
"6396","Ali (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade every fanged beast of prey, every taloned bird, the price of carrion, flesh of domestic donkeys, the dowry of the adulteress, stud fees for the stallion, and purple saddle-cloths. > **""Every fanged beast of prey, every taloned bird, the price of carrion, the flesh of domestic donkeys, the dowry of the adulteress, the stud fee of the stallion, and purple saddle-cloths are forbidden.""**"
"6397","Al-Bara' ibn Azib (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ the prohibition of the price of the dog, the dowry of the adulteress, the earnings of the cupper, the sweet of the soothsayer, and the stud fee of the stallion. Bara' himself had a he-goat which he lent freely to whoever sought it for covering, refusing none and taking no stud wage. > **""The price of the dog, the dowry of the adulteress, the earnings of the cupper, the sweet of the soothsayer, and the stud fee of the stallion are forbidden.""**"
"6398","Al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted among suht, unlawful earnings, the price of the dog, the dowry of the adulteress, and the earnings of the cupper. > **""Among suht are: the price of the dog, the dowry of the adulteress, and the earnings of the cupper.""**"
"6399","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) stated that the dowry of the adulteress, the wage of the soothsayer, the earnings of the cupper, and the price of the dog are disliked and unlawful."
"6400","Abd al-Wahid al-Bunani reported that he was with Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) when a man who had bought vineyards with grapes, unable to sell all as grapes and needing to press some, asked about the price of wine. Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) replied by narrating that they sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he ﷺ looked to the sky, lowered himself and scratched the earth, saying woe to the Children of Israel; and when Umar (رضي الله عنه) was alarmed, he ﷺ explained they melted forbidden fats, sold them and consumed the price, and likewise wine's price is unlawful for you. > **""Woe to the Children of Israel. There is no harm upon you from that. When fats were forbidden to them, they melted them, sold them, and consumed their price; and likewise the price of wine is unlawful for you.""**"
"6401","Kaysan (رضي الله عنه) reported that he traded in wine in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and came from Syria with wine in skins for trade. When he offered excellent drink to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he ﷺ informed him it had been forbidden after him, and that both it and its price were forbidden; so Kaysan went to the skins, seized them by their legs, and poured them out. > **""O Kaysan, it has been forbidden after you. It has been forbidden, and its price has been forbidden.""**"
"6402","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm (رضي الله عنه) reported that al-Dari used to gift the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a camel-load of wine each year; in the year it was forbidden he brought a load, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ smiled and asked whether he knew it had been forbidden after him, and when he asked whether he might sell it and benefit by its price, he ﷺ thrice cursed the Jews for melting forbidden sheep and cattle fats, shaping them likewise, selling them and consuming: wine and its price are thrice unlawful. > **""Did you know it has been forbidden after you? May Allah curse the Jews, may Allah curse the Jews, may Allah curse the Jews; they turned to what was forbidden to them of sheep and cattle fats, melted them, formed them likewise, and sold that by which they eat. Wine is unlawful and its price is unlawful; wine is unlawful and its price is unlawful; wine is unlawful and its price is unlawful.""**"
"6403","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm (رضي الله عنه) reported from Tamim al-Dari (رضي الله عنه) the same account of yearly gifting, in abridged form, with the ruling on its purchase and price. > **""Its purchase is unlawful and its price is unlawful.""**"
"6404","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man used to carry wine from Khaybar to Medina and sell it to the Muslims for wealth. When a Muslim met him and told him wine had been forbidden, he set it upon a mound covered with garments, came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking confirmation, asked whether he might return it to its seller or gift it for recompense, and mentioned orphans' wealth in it; he ﷺ disallowed return and gifting, promised compensation from Bahrayn wealth, then proclaimed to Medina, and when asked whether the skins could be used, commanded their mouths loosed so it poured until it settled in the valley's belly. > **""Yes. Its return is not proper. No. When the wealth of Bahrayn comes to us, come to us and we shall compensate your orphans from their wealth. O people of Medina, loose its bindings.""**"
"6405","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man of Thaqif gifted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a camel-load of wine after its prohibition, so he ﷺ ordered it split open. When a man suggested ordering its sale, he ﷺ answered that its forbiddance covers both uses. > **""He who forbade its drinking forbade its selling.""**"
"6406","Yahya ibn Abbad reported that a skin of wine was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ after its prohibition; when it was brought he ﷺ declared wine forbidden. When some suggested selling it and giving its price to the Muslim poor, he ﷺ ordered it poured out in a valley of Medina, cursing the Jews for selling what was forbidden of fats and consuming their prices. > **""Wine has been forbidden. May Allah curse the Jews: their fats were forbidden to them, so they sold them and consumed their prices.""**"
"6407","Amir ibn Rabi'ah reported from his father (رضي الله عنه) that a man of Thaqif called Abu Tammam gifted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a camel-load of wine. He ﷺ told him it had been forbidden, and when he asked whether he might spend its price, he ﷺ answered with the parity of drinking and price. > **""It has been forbidden, O Abu Tammam. He who forbade its drinking forbade its price.""**"
"6408","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed wine, its drinker, its pourer, its presser, the one for whom it is pressed, its carrier, the one to whom it is carried, its seller, its buyer, and the consumer of its price. > **""May Allah curse wine, its drinker, its pourer, its presser, the one for whom it is pressed, its carrier, the one to whom it is carried, its seller, its buyer, and the consumer of its price.""**"
"6409","Al-Hasan reported that a freedman of Uthman ibn Abi al-As (رضي الله عنه) asked for capital to trade with shared profit, received twenty thousand dirhams, bought wine with it, and brought it to al-Ubullah. Uthman (رضي الله عنه) went out to him, broke every vat and vessel, and cited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursing wine and its drinker, buyer, seller, presser, carrier, the one for whom it is pressed, the one to whom it is carried, and the consumer of its price. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed wine, its drinker, its buyer, its seller, its presser, the one for whom it is pressed, its carrier, the one to whom it is carried, and the consumer of its price.""**"
"6410","It is reported from al-Hasan that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade wine and declared its price unlawful. > **""Wine is forbidden, and its price is unlawful.""**"
"6411","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) stated that Allah cursed wine, its presser, its drinker, its pourer, its seller, its buyer, its carrier, the one to whom it is carried, and the consumer of its price."
"6412","Amir ibn Rabi'ah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he gifted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a camel-load of wine, and he ﷺ asked whether he knew it had been forbidden after him. When he asked whether he might sell it to the Jews, he ﷺ equated its seller with its drinker, so he poured it out. > **""O Amir, did you not know it has been forbidden after you? Its seller is like its drinker.""**"
"6413","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that when the prohibition of wine descended, they asked whether they might sell, and he ﷺ answered with the parity of drinking and sale. > **""He who forbade its drinking forbade its selling.""**"
"6414","Umm Sulaym (رضي الله عنها) reported that when the prohibition of wine descended, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded a crier to proclaim that wine had been forbidden, so none should sell or buy it, and whoever had any should pour it out. Abu Talhah (رضي الله عنه) ordered his lad to loose the skins, and the people poured out, though their wine then was only from unripe and ripe dates. > **""Indeed wine has been forbidden, so do not sell it, nor buy it; and whoever has any of it, let him pour it out.""**"
"6415","Buraydah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned one who withholds grapes through harvest days to sell to a Jew, Christian, or winemaker that he plunges knowingly into the Fire. > **""Whoever withholds grapes during harvest days until he sells them to a Jew, or a Christian, or one who makes them wine, has plunged into the Fire knowingly.""**"
"6416","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Mecca in the year of the Conquest declare Allah and His Messenger's prohibition of selling wine, carrion, and swine. When asked about carrion fats used for caulking ships, greasing skins, and lighting, he ﷺ said no, it is unlawful, then invoked Allah's curse on the Jews for melting forbidden fats, selling them, and consuming their price. > **""Allah and His Messenger have forbidden selling wine, carrion, and swine. No, it is unlawful. May Allah destroy the Jews: when Allah forbade them fats, they melted them, sold them, and consumed their price.""**"
"6417","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Mecca he declared unlawful drinking wine and its price, eating carrion and its price, swine, eating it and its price; commanded trimming moustaches, sparing beards, and wearing the waist-wrapper in markets; and disowned imitation of others' ways. > **""Allah and His Messenger have forbidden you drinking wine and its price, and have forbidden you eating carrion and its price, and have forbidden you swine, eating it and its price. Trim the moustaches, spare the beards, and do not walk in the markets except with the waist-wrapper upon you; he is not of us who acts upon the way of other than us.""**"
"6418","A'ishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared unlawful the singing girl, her sale, her price, her teaching, and listening to her. > **""Allah has forbidden the singing girl, her sale, her price, her teaching, and listening to her.""**"
"6419","Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the price of the singing girl suht, her singing and gazing upon her unlawful, her price like the price of the dog, the dog's price suht, and the Fire most deserving of flesh grown on suht. > **""The price of the singing girl is suht, her singing is unlawful, gazing at her is unlawful, her price is like the price of the dog, the price of the dog is suht, and whoever's flesh grows upon suht, the Fire is more deserving of him.""**"
"6420","Ali (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling, buying, and trading in singing and wailing women, declaring their earnings unlawful. > **""Their earnings are unlawful.""**"
"6421","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ the prohibition of the price of the dog, describing it as a provision of Jahiliyyah. > **""The price of the dog is forbidden; it is a provision of Jahiliyyah.""**"
"6422","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the price of the dog and the dowry of the adulteress. > **""The price of the dog and the dowry of the adulteress are forbidden.""**"
"6423","Ubadah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when asked about the prices of dogs, answered that they are Jahili provision from which Allah has sufficed. > **""It is the food of the people of Jahiliyyah, and Allah the Exalted has sufficed beyond it.""**"
"6424","Maymunah bint Sa'd (رضي الله عنها) reported that she asked for a ruling about dogs, and he ﷺ answered that they are Jahili provision from which Allah has sufficed. > **""It is a provision of Jahiliyyah, and Allah the Exalted has sufficed beyond it.""**"
"6425","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared unlawful both the price of the stolen sheep, al-harisah, and its eating. > **""The price of the harisah is unlawful, and eating it is unlawful.""**"
"6426","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that on the Day of the Trench a polytheist was struck down, his people asked the Prophet ﷺ to let them bury him, he ﷺ refused them honour, and when they offered payment for it, he declared it doubly foul. > **""No, nor honour for you. That is fouler and fouler.""**"
"6427","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) reported that they set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey in companies, he lodging with Abu Bakr's (رضي الله عنه) company alongside a desert Bedouin. They alighted among desert dwellers with a pregnant woman; the Bedouin promised her a boy if she gave him a sheep, took a sheep, chanted rhymed incantations for her, then slaughtered it. When the eaters learned its origin from an informant, Abu Bakr (رضي الله عنه) withdrew, purged himself, and vomited."
"6428","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported in marfu' form the prohibition of seeking inflated yield from a slave-girl, except one skilled with her hands. > **""Do not seek profit from a slave-girl, except a slave-girl skilled with her hands.""**"
"6429","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the earnings of the slave-girl unless she has a known, established craft. > **""The earnings of the slave-girl are forbidden, unless she has a lasting, known craft.""**"
"6430","A'ishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded not lodging women in upper chambers, not teaching them writing, but teaching them spinning and Surat al-Nur. > **""Do not lodge them in upper rooms, nor teach them writing; teach them spinning and Surat al-Nur.""**"
"6431","Ziyad ibn Abdillah al-Qurashi reported that he entered upon Hind bint al-Muhallab ibn Abi Sufrah, wife of al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf, while she held a spindle spinning, and when he marvelled at her spinning as a commander's wife, she cited her father from her grandfather that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the longest in capacity among them is greatest in reward. > **""The longest of you in capacity is the greatest of you in reward.""**"
"6432","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the earnings of the cupper. > **""The earnings of the cupper are forbidden.""**"
"6433","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say he had gifted his maternal aunt Fakhitah bint Amr (رضي الله عنها) a slave boy and ordered her not to make him a butcher, craftsman, or cupper. > **""I have gifted my maternal aunt Fakhitah bint Amr a slave boy, and ordered her not to make him a butcher, nor a craftsman, nor a cupper.""**"
"6434","A man of the Ansar called Muhayyisah (رضي الله عنه) had a cupper slave, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rebuked him over his earnings. When he asked whether he might feed his orphans with it or give it in charity, he ﷺ twice said no, then permitted him to use it as fodder for his water-carrying camel. > **""No. No. Fodder with it your water-carrying camel.""**"
"6435","Yahya ibn Abi Sulaym reports that he heard 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' relate that when his grandfather died, he left behind a slave-girl, a water-carrying camel (nadih), a slave boy who was a cupper, and a piece of land. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave rulings concerning each: regarding the slave-girl he forbade her earnings — Shu'bah added, for fear she would seek them through immorality — and regarding the cupper he instructed that his earnings be spent as fodder, and regarding the land he instructed that it be cultivated or left alone. > **""Feed the water-camel with what the cupper earns. Cultivate it (the land), or leave it idle.""**"
"6436","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about the earnings of the cupper, and he directed that they be spent as animal feed. > **""Feed it to your water-camel.""**"
"6437","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned Abu Taybah, who cupped him. He then asked him about the amount of his imposed tax, and when he replied that it was three sa', he interceded so that one sa' was remitted from him. > **""How much is your tax (daribah)?""**"
"6438","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was cupped in the two jugular veins (al-akhda'ayn) and between the shoulders, and he gave the cupper his wage — and had it been unlawful, he would not have given it to him."
"6439","Abu Jamilah al-Tuhawi reports that he heard 'Ali رضي الله عنه say that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was cupped, and when the cupper finished, he asked him about the amount of his kharaj. When he replied that it was two sa', he remitted one sa' from him and instructed 'Ali to give him one sa'. > **""How much is your kharaj?""**"
"6440","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped, and the cupper complained to him about his imposed tax, so he sent word to his masters that they should lighten his tax from him."
"6441","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped and gave the cupper a dinar as his wage."
"6442","Thawban رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was cupped and gave the cupper his wage, instructing that it be used as animal feed. > **""Feed it to your water-camel.""**"
"6443","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports: While we were reciting — among us an Arab and a non-Arab, a Black man and a White man — the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us and declared that we were in a good state, reciting the Book of Allah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among us, but that people would come after who would straighten and embellish it as an arrow-shaft is straightened, hastening to take its wages and not deferring them. > **""You are in a good state, reciting the Book of Allah, and among you is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. But there will come people who will craft it (the recitation) as the arrow-shaft is crafted, hastening its wages and not deferring them.""**"
"6444","Anas رضي الله عنه reports in another narration that the Prophet ﷺ — meaning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — came out to us and said that among us was one better than us, meaning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself, and that we were reciting the Book of Allah with red and white, non-Arab and Arab among us, then he mentioned the like of the previous narration. > **""Among you is one better than you, and you recite the Book of Allah — among you the red, the white, the non-Arab, and the Arab.""**"
"6445","Abu Sallam reports that Mu'awiyah رضي الله عنه wrote to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl رضي الله عنه to teach the people what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. So he gathered them and said that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command learning the Qur'an, and warn against going to excess in it, abandoning it, eating by it, or seeking abundance through it. > **""Learn the Qur'an, and when you have learned it, do not go to excess in it, nor turn away from it in neglect, nor eat by it, nor seek increase through it.""**"
"6446","Al-Tufayl ibn 'Amr al-Dawsi رضي الله عنه reports that Ubayy ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه taught him the Qur'an, so he gifted him a bow. The next morning Ubayy went to the Prophet ﷺ wearing it slung around his neck, and the Prophet ﷺ warned him that he had slung upon himself a portion of Hell. The narrator then asked about attending people's meals and eating from them, and the Prophet ﷺ distinguished between food prepared for one's sake and food one merely happens upon. > **""You have slung upon yourself (a bow) from Hell. As for what was prepared for you, you eat it at the expense of your (Hereafter) share; but as for what was prepared for other than you, and you attended and ate from it, there is no harm in it.""**"
"6447","Isma'il ibn 'Ubaydullah reports that 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan said to him: O Isma'il, discipline my sons, for I shall pay you. He replied: How can that be, when Umm al-Darda' رضي الله عنها narrated to me from Abu al-Darda' رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, that whoever takes a bow in return for teaching the Qur'an, Allah will collar him with a bow of Fire. > **""Whoever takes a bow in return for teaching the Qur'an, Allah will collar him with a bow of Fire.""**"
"6448","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reports that an expedition dispatched by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by an Arab tribe who asked whether their master had come with light and healing. When they confirmed it, the tribesmen mentioned a man possessed, confused by Satan. A man of the Ansar had him brought, recited the Fatihah of the Book over him three times, and the man recovered, so they drove sheep to the Companions. Some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ objected that it was not lawful to take a wage for the Qur'an, while others said it was but an honour bestowed, not a wage for the Qur'an. Some ate from the slaughtered animal and some abstained until they could ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon return. When they returned, the one to whom the sheep had been given recounted the events, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked what had taught him that it was a ruqyah, affirmed that he had recited from the Speech of Allah, and declared his earning from a true ruqyah lawful, permitting him to eat and feed his companions. > **""What taught you that it is a ruqyah? Whoever gains by a false ruqyah, you have indeed gained by a true ruqyah. Eat, and feed your companions.""**"
"6449","'Awf ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that a man who was learning the Qur'an from him bought a bow and gifted it to him. When he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether he might take it, the Prophet ﷺ refused. He waited until a year had passed and asked again, and was again refused; then again after another year, and was again refused. When he suggested taking it as equipment in the path of Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned him of meeting Allah with an ember of Hell between his shoulders. > **""No. Do you wish, O 'Awf, to meet Allah on the Day of Resurrection with an ember of Hell between your shoulders?""**"
"6450","Al-Muthanna ibn Wa'il reports that he visited 'Abdullah ibn Bishr رضي الله عنه, who wiped his head, and a man asked him about the wage of the teacher. He replied that a man wearing a bow slung over his shoulder once entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the bow pleased the Prophet ﷺ, and when he asked whether he had bought it, the man replied that it had been gifted to him by one whose son he had taught the Qur'an. The Prophet ﷺ then warned him of being collared with a bow of Fire and ordered it returned. > **""How fine is your bow — did you buy it? Would you love that Allah collars you with a bow of Fire? Then return it.""**"
"6451","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that among the captives of Badr were some who had no ransom to pay, so the Prophet ﷺ fixed their ransom as teaching writing to the children of the Ansar. One day a boy came weeping to his father, complaining that his teacher had beaten him, and his father replied that the wretched man was seeking revenge for Badr, swearing by Allah that the boy would never go to him again."
"6452","'Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i رضي الله عنه reports: We campaigned with 'Amr ibn al-'As رضي الله عنه, and with us were 'Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه and Abu 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah رضي الله عنه. Severe hunger struck us, and I found people intending to slaughter a camel. I offered to help them slaughter it in return for a share of it, and they agreed. When that was mentioned to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه, he said: You have hastened your reward, and I shall not eat of it — and Abu 'Ubaydah said the like. Then he went forward to the Prophet ﷺ, and when the Prophet ﷺ saw him, he remarked upon him. > **""Is this the companion of the camel?!""**"
"6453","'Awf ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ, in a narration similar to one to come, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him on an expedition. A man said he would go out with him if a share of the booty were assigned to him, then added that he did not know whether they would gain any booty at all, so a fixed share should be assigned. 'Awf fixed three dinars for him. They campaigned and gained booty, and when he asked the Prophet ﷺ about the matter, the Prophet ﷺ declared that nothing of this world or the Hereafter was lawful for him beyond the three dinars he had taken. > **""Nothing is lawful for him in this world or the Hereafter except these three dinars of his which he took.""**"
"6454","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade hiring a labourer until his wage has been made clear to him. > **""Do not hire a labourer until his wage has been made clear to him.""**"
"6455","'Ali رضي الله عنه reports: I once suffered severe hunger in Madinah, so I went out seeking work in the outskirts of Madinah. There I found a woman who had gathered unbaked bricks, and I thought she wanted them wetted. So I contracted with her for one date for every bucket (dhanub) of water. I drew sixteen buckets until my hands were blistered, then I drank from the water, came to her, and held out my hands thus — Isma'il ibn Ibrahim spread and joined his hands to show it — and she counted out sixteen dates to me. I then came to the Prophet ﷺ, informed him, and he ate with me from them."
"6456","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the labourer should be given his wage before his perspiration dries. > **""Give the labourer his wage before his sweat dries.""**"
"6457","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the labourer should be given his wage before his sweat dries. > **""Give the labourer his wage before his sweat dries.""**"
"6458","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the worker should be given from his work, for the worker of Allah does not fail. > **""Give the worker from his work, for the worker of Allah does not fail.""**"
"6459","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the best earning is the earning of the worker's hand when he is sincere. > **""The best earning is the earning of the worker's hand when he is sincere.""**"
"6460","'A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that Allah loves that when one of you does a work, he perfects it. > **""Indeed, Allah loves that when one of you does a work, he perfects it.""**"
"6461","'Asim ibn Kulayb reports from his father that he went out with his father to a funeral attended by the Prophet ﷺ — and he was then a discerning boy — and the Prophet ﷺ said that Allah loves the worker when he works to perfect his work. > **""Allah loves the worker when he works, that he perfects it.""**"
"6462","Sirin reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a gap in a grave and ordered that it be filled. When it was asked whether that would benefit the deceased, he replied that it would neither benefit nor harm him, but it comforts the eye of the living. > **""It neither benefits him nor harms him, but it comforts the eye of the living.""**"
"6463","Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab reports that he heard 'Uthman رضي الله عنه saying, while delivering the khutbah upon the minbar: I used to buy dates from a clan of the Jews called Banu Qaynuqa', buying them for profit, and that reached the Prophet ﷺ, who instructed him to measure fully when buying and to measure out when selling. > **""O 'Uthman, when you buy, measure fully (for yourself), and when you sell, measure out (for others).""**"
"6464","'Umar رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever buys food should not sell it until he has taken it in full. > **""Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he has taken it in full.""**"
"6465","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he forbade selling food until the two sa'-measures have run their course in it — so that the increase belongs to its owner and the loss is upon him. > **""Do not sell food until the two sa'-measures have passed in it, so that the increase belongs to its owner and the loss is upon him.""**"
"6466","Simawayh reports: I came to the Prophet ﷺ and heard with my own ears — we used to carry wheat from al-Balqa' to Madinah, sell it, and intend to buy dates from Madinah in return, but we were prevented. So we came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, and the Prophet ﷺ said to those who had prevented us that the cheapness of this food against the expensiveness of these dates they carried should suffice them, and to leave them to carry it. Simawayh was a Christian sexton (shammas) from al-Balqa'; he accepted Islam, his Islam was excellent, and he lived one hundred and twenty years. > **""Does not the cheapness of this food suffice you against the expensiveness of these dates which they carry? Leave them to carry it.""**"
"6467","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reports: Prices rose in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they said to him: If only you would fix our prices for us. He replied that Allah is the Fixer — or the Pricer — and that he hoped to part from them with none of them seeking from him redress for an injustice in wealth or life. > **""Indeed, Allah is the Fixer (of prices) — or the Pricer. I hope to part from you with none of you seeking from me redress for an injustice in wealth or life.""**"
"6468","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, fix prices for us. He replied that he would rather supplicate Allah. Then another man came and said: O Messenger of Allah, fix prices for us. He replied that Allah raises and lowers, and that he hoped to meet Allah with no injustice owed to anyone with him. > **""Rather I shall supplicate Allah. Rather, Allah raises and lowers, and I hope to meet Allah with no injustice owed to anyone with me.""**"
"6469","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports: Prices rose in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they said: O Messenger of Allah, fix prices for us. He replied that Allah is the Pricer, the Withholder, the Extender, and that he hoped to meet Allah with none of them seeking from him redress for an injustice in honour or wealth. > **""Indeed, Allah is the Pricer, the Withholder, the Extender, and I hope to meet Allah with none of you seeking from me redress for an injustice in honour or wealth.""**"
"6470","'Ali — meaning 'Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه — reports that it was said: O Messenger of Allah, fix prices for us. He replied that the rise and cheapness of prices is in the Hand of Allah, and that he wished to meet his Lord with none seeking from him redress for an injustice he had done to him. > **""The rise and cheapness of prices is in the Hand of Allah. I wish to meet my Lord with none seeking from me redress for an injustice I did to him.""**"
"6471","Abu Juhayfah رضي الله عنه reports that they said: O Messenger of Allah, fix prices for us. He replied that Allah is the Pricer, the Withholder, the Extender, and that he hoped to meet Allah with none of them seeking from him redress for an injustice in honour or wealth. > **""Indeed, Allah is the Pricer, the Withholder, the Extender, and I hope to meet Allah with none of you seeking from me redress for an injustice in honour or wealth.""**"
"6472","Abu Busaylah رضي الله عنه reports that in the sixth year it was said to the Prophet ﷺ: Fix prices for us, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied that Allah would not question him about a practice he had innovated upon them which He had not commanded him with, but that they should ask Allah of His bounty. > **""Allah will not question me about a practice I innovated upon you which He did not command me with. Rather, ask Allah of His bounty.""**"
"6473","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the two sellers retain the option regarding their sale so long as they have not parted, or their sale is one stipulated with an option. > **""The two sellers retain the option regarding their sale so long as they have not parted, or their sale is with an option.""**"
"6474","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded a sale with a man, then said to him to choose, and then said: Such is the sale. > **""Choose. Such is the sale.""**"
"6475","'Abdullah ibn Qays al-Aslami رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bought from a man of Banu Ghifar two shares at Khaybar in exchange for a slave. At the time of the sale, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him that what they had taken from him was better than what they had given him, and that regarding what he would take from him — if he wished he might take it, and if he wished he might leave it. > **""Know that what we took from you is better than what we gave you, and as for what you take from me — if you wish, take it, and if you wish, leave it.""**"
"6476","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever hoards food for forty days has disowned Allah and Allah has disowned him, and whichever neighbourhood wakes in the morning with a hungry man among them, the protection of Allah has been lifted from them. > **""Whoever hoards food for forty days has disowned Allah, Blessed and Exalted, and Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has disowned him. And whichever neighbourhood wakes with a hungry man among them, the protection of Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has been lifted from them.""**"
"6477","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever hoards a hoard intending thereby to raise prices against the Muslims is a wrongdoer. > **""Whoever hoards a hoard intending to raise prices thereby against the Muslims is a wrongdoer (khati').""**"
"6478","Al-Hasan reports that Ma'qil ibn Yasar رضي الله عنه grew gravely ill, and 'Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad came to visit him. He asked: Do you know, O Ma'qil, that I have shed unlawful blood? He replied: I do not know of it. He asked: Do you know that I have interfered in any of the prices of the Muslims? He replied: I do not know of it. Ma'qil then said: Seat me up, and said: Listen, O 'Ubaydullah, until I narrate to you something I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — not once, nor twice — that whoever interferes in any of the prices of the Muslims in order to raise them against them, it is due upon Allah to seat him in a mighty portion of the Fire on the Day of Resurrection. 'Ubaydullah asked: Did you yourself hear it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He replied: Yes — not once, nor twice. > **""Whoever interferes in any of the prices of the Muslims in order to raise them against them, it is due upon Allah, Blessed and Exalted, to seat him in a mighty portion of the Fire on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6479","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that hoarding food in Makkah is ilhad (sacrilegious deviation). > **""Hoarding food in Makkah is ilhad.""**"
"6480","Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what hoarding (ihtikar) is. He replied that it is when one is displeased to hear of cheapness and rejoices to hear of expensiveness — evil is the hoarding servant: if Allah cheapens prices he grieves, and if He raises them he rejoices. > **""When he hears of cheapness it displeases him, and when he hears of expensiveness he rejoices at it. Evil is the hoarding servant: if Allah cheapens prices he grieves, and if He raises them he rejoices.""**"
"6481","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade, on the day of Hunayn, the sale of the khums (one-fifth of the booty) before it had been divided."
"6482","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the selling of the shares (of booty) before they had been divided."
"6483","'Imran ibn Hayyan al-Ansari reports from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a khutbah on the day of Khaybar, forbidding them to sell any share of booty before it was divided, to have relations with pregnant captives until they had given birth, and to sell fruit before its ripeness had appeared and it was safe from blight. Duhaym added in his narration that he then permitted them three things he had previously forbidden: the meat of sacrificial animals, the visitation of graves, and vessels."
"6484","Al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman reports that 'Ali رضي الله عنه and Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه used to permit the sale of sadaqah before it had been taken into possession, while Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه and Shurayh would not permit it until it had been taken into possession — and the view of Mu'adh and Shurayh was more beloved to Sufyan."
"6485","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling fruit before it is edible, selling wool while it is still on the back (of the animal), and selling milk while it is still in the udder."
"6486","'A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports from the Prophet ﷺ that they should not sell their fruits until their soundness has appeared and they are safe from blight. > **""Do not sell your fruits until their soundness has appeared and they are safe from blight.""**"
"6487","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that fruit should not be sold until its soundness has appeared. When asked what its soundness was, he explained that its blight departs and its soundness becomes pure. > **""Do not sell fruit until its soundness has appeared. Its blight departs and its soundness becomes pure.""**"
"6488","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling fruit before it is edible."
"6489","In another narration, he forbade the sale of dates until their soundness has appeared."
"6490","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that fruit should not be sold until its soundness has appeared. > **""Do not sell fruit until its soundness has appeared.""**"
"6491","Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid the owner of palm-trees from taking on debt against the fruit of his palms before its fruit could be eaten — fearing he would take on a large debt and then the fruit would spoil and he could not repay it. He used to forbid the owner of a crop from taking debt against his crop before harvest time, and he used to forbid the owner of gold, when he sold it for food consisting of fruit, from selling the food for gold before measuring the food and taking it into possession, for fear of riba."
"6492","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when the star rises in the morning, blight is lifted. > **""When the star rises in the morning, blight is lifted.""**"
"6493","In another narration: Never does the star rise in the morning while a blight is upon a people except that it is lifted — or dries up. In the wording of al-Tabarani in al-Saghir: When the star ascends, blight is lifted from every land. > **""Never does the star rise in the morning while a blight is upon a people except that it is lifted — or dries up. When the star ascends, blight is lifted from every land.""**"
"6494","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that there is no harm in a man selling his 'ariyyah from the palm-trees for its estimated measure in dates, intending that the other eat it. > **""There is no harm in a man selling his 'ariyyah from the palm-trees for its estimate in dates, intending that the other eat it.""**"
"6495","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave concession for 'araya in the amount of one wasq, two wasqs, three, and four, saying that for every harvest ten awsuq are due, and whatever remains as clusters is to be placed in the mosque for the poor. Muhammad said: Today they stipulate that upon the traders. > **""In every harvest, ten awsuq; and whatever remains as clusters is to be placed in the mosque for the poor.""**"
"6496","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports — as Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash understood it — that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade (the exchange of) fresh dates for dry dates and grapes for raisins, but gave concession for 'araya. 'Araya is when a Bedouin comes to a cousin of his or a man of his household, who assigns him one palm-tree or two before they have ripened, and he wishes to depart — then there is no harm in his selling it for dates."
"6497","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade muhaqalah and muzabanah. 'Ikrimah used to dislike the sale of the fasil (the unborn young)."
"6498","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه said: Salam (advance payment) is not valid in unhusked wheat, barley, and sult until they are threshed, nor in grapes, olives and their likes until they sweeten, nor gold in hand for silver as a debt, nor silver as a debt for gold in hand."
"6499","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling palm-trees for two or three years (of future fruit), buying what is on the palm-tops for a fixed measure, or selling fruit before its soundness has appeared."
"6500","Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the sale of two years (i.e. selling fruit two years in advance)."
"6501","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the sale of al-madamin (what is in the wombs of females), al-malaqih (what is in the loins of males), and habal al-habalah (the offspring of the offspring)."
"6502","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the sale of al-malaqih and al-madamin."
"6503","'Ubayd ibn Nadlah al-Khuza'i رضي الله عنه reports: Severe hardship struck the people in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A man divided his camel into tenths, then said: Whoever wishes may take a tenth of this meat in exchange for a young she-camel (qalus) due at habal al-habalah (when the pregnant one's offspring gives birth)? People took it, and when that reached the Prophet ﷺ, he ordered that it be returned — so the sale was revoked."
"6504","'Ubayd ibn Nadlah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade habal al-habalah — that is, what is in the belly of the she-camel."
"6505","'Ubayd ibn Nadlah al-Khuza'i رضي الله عنه reports that a man slaughtered a camel, and another man bought a tenth of it in exchange for a huqqah (a container / young camel). When that reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he revoked it. Abu Nu'aym said: Some of our companions said in it, from Sufyan: (on credit) until a fixed term."
"6506","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade selling meat in exchange for a live animal."
"6507","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade selling an animal for an animal on credit (nasi'ah)."
"6508","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade selling an animal for an animal on credit."
"6509","Jabir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling an animal for an animal on credit."
"6510","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade taking one dinar for two, one dirham for two, and one sa' for two, warning them of riba. When asked about a horse sold for several horses or a noble she-camel for camels, he ﷺ permitted it on condition of immediate hand-to-hand exchange. > **""Do not take one dinar for two dinars, nor one dirham for two dirhams, nor one sa' for two sa's. I fear riba for you. There is no harm in a horse for horses or a noble she-camel for camels when it is hand to hand.""**"
"6511","Al-Sunabihi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw an aged she-camel among the sadaqah camels and became angry, asking whence it came. The keeper explained he had taken it in return for two inferior camels from the Fringes of the sadaqah herd, at which the Prophet ﷺ fell silent, and in one narration approved it. > **""What is this? — Then yes, that is fine.""**"
"6512","Aswad ibn Asram, may Allah be pleased with him, came to Madinah in a year of drought with fat camels that amazed its people. The Prophet ﷺ summoned him, asked his purpose, and when he sought a servant in exchange, 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, produced one whom Aswad accepted, the Prophet ﷺ taking possession of the camels. Aswad then sought counsel, and was enjoined to master his tongue and hand for good alone. > **""Control your tongue. Control your hand. Speak with your tongue only what is good, and extend your hand only toward good.""**"
"6513","Abyad ibn Hammal, may Allah be pleased with him, embraced Islam while owning three Kindah brothers as slaves from Jahiliyyah. In the caliphate of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, he came as a delegate; Abu Bakr asked him to free the one who served him and to sell the warrior brothers for six non-Arab captives of Qadisiyyah. Abyad did so, freeing those with him and taking six in their place. The report also notes his treaty of seventy robes confirmed by the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr. > **""Free the neck of the one who serves you, and sell me his brothers who fight, for six of the captives of Qadisiyyah.""**"
"6514","Yazid ibn Abi Nu'aym reports that in Jahiliyyah a man of Aslam named 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr fathered a boy called al-Hammam by a slave-girl. His uncle came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pleading for the son, was told to recover him as best he could, and seized him. When the boy's master came disputing, the Prophet ﷺ offered him two slaves in compensation to leave the father his son, and he took one. > **""Recover your son as best you can. Take one of the two slaves and leave the man's son to him.""**"
"6515","'Abdullah ibn 'Umar and Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them, report the ruling that a slave's property and the liability for his debt remain with the seller unless the buyer stipulates otherwise, and likewise the fruit of a pollinated palm remains the seller's unless the buyer stipulates otherwise. > **""Whoever sells a slave who possesses property, his property belongs to the seller and his debt is upon the seller, unless the buyer stipulates otherwise. Whoever pollinates palms and then sells them after pollination, its fruit belongs to him, unless the buyer stipulates otherwise.""**"
"6516","'Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports the ruling that one who sells a slave possessing property while debt lies upon him leaves the debt upon the seller, unless the seller stipulates it upon the buyer. > **""Whoever sells a slave who has property and upon whom is a debt, the debt is upon the seller unless the seller stipulates it upon the buyer.""**"
"6517","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ limited the warranty of a slave to four days and affirmed the option of the two contracting parties so long as they have not separated. > **""There is no warranty after four days, and the two sellers retain the option so long as they have not parted.""**"
"6518","'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to sell two slave brothers. He sold them separately and mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ, who ordered him to overtake them, take them back, and sell them only together. > **""Overtake them and take them back, and do not sell them except together.""**"
"6519","Dumayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed by Umm Dumayrah weeping because she had been separated from her son. He forbade separating mother and child, had her returned through the chain of buyers, then purchased the household himself and freed them, granting them a written covenant that they were an Arab household free to remain with him ﷺ or return to their people, to be met only with good. > **""A mother is not to be separated from her child. In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful: from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Abu Dumayrah and his household — the Messenger of Allah has freed them. They are an Arab household; if they wish they may stay with the Messenger of Allah, and if they wish they may return to their people, and none shall confront them except with good.""**"
"6520","وَعَنْ مَعْقِلِ بْنِ يَسَارٍ قَالَ: قَالَ رَسُولُ اللَّهِ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ -: «مَنْ فَرَّقَ فَلَيْسَ» قَالَ: أَشَدُّ تَفَرُّقٍ الْوَلَدُ وَأَمُّهُ، وَبَيْنَ الْإِخْوَةِ. Ma'qil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports the stern warning against splitting kinsmen in sale, explaining that the severest form is between a child and his mother and between brothers. > **""Whoever separates, he is not of us. The severest separation is between a child and his mother, and between brothers.""**"
"6521","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, recalls that they used to buy and sell ummahat al-awlad — slave-girls who had borne children to their masters — while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among them. > **""We used to trade in ummahat al-awlad while the Messenger of Allah was among us.""**"
"6522","Zayd ibn Wahb reports that when a man died leaving an umm walad and al-Walid ibn 'Uqbah wished to sell her for the dead man's debt, they consulted Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, after his prayer. He ruled that if sale were unavoidable, she should be placed within her own child's share so as not to be alienated from him."
"6523","وَعَنْ عَلْقَمَةَ قَالَ: جَاءَ رَجُلٌ إِلَى ابْنِ مَسْعُودٍ، فَقَالَ: إِنَّ جَارِيَةً لِي قَدْ أَرْضَعَتِ ابْنًا لِي، وَأَنَا أُرِيدُ أَنْ أَبِيعَهَا فَمَقَتَهُ ابْنُ مَسْعُودٍ، وَقَالَ: لَيْتَهُ يُنَادِي: مَنْ أَبِيعُهُ أُمَّ وَلَدِي؟. 'Alqamah reports that a man told Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, that his slave-girl had suckled his son and he intended to sell her. Ibn Mas'ud expressed revulsion, deeming it akin to proclaiming the sale of the mother of his child."
"6524","عَنْ عِمْرَانَ بْنِ حُصَيْنٍ «أَنَّ رَسُولَ اللَّهِ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ - نَهَى عَنْ بَيْعِ السِّلَاحِ فِي الْفِتْنَةِ». 'Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the sale of arms during civil strife, lest they fuel bloodshed among Muslims. > **""Do not sell weapons in times of fitnah.""**"
"6525","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the sale of muhaffalat — animals whose milk is deliberately retained in the udder to deceive buyers — and granted one who buys them the option to rescind after milking them and discovering the deceit. > **""Whoever buys muhaffalat has the option when he milks them.""**"
"6526","Abu Layla, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ ruled that one who buys a musarrat she-camel — one whose udder was tied to inflate its yield — may return her with a sa' of dates if displeased. > **""Whoever buys a musarrat she-camel, if he dislikes her let him return her along with a sa' of dates.""**"
"6527","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade tying up the udders of camels and sheep for sale to inflate their milk. The buyer of a musarrat sheep has two considerations — to keep or return — and if he returns her, he returns a sa' of dates with her. > **""Do not tie up the udders of camels and sheep for sale. Whoever buys a musarrat sheep has one of two choices; if he returns her, let him return her with a sa' of dates.""**"
"6528","Salamah ibn al-Akwa', may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade leaving camels to wander untended without shepherd or restraint, commanding their udders to be tied, for Satan suckles what is left exposed. > **""Do not send out camels neglected. Tie their udders firmly, for Satan suckles them.""**"
"6529","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ counselled 'Abdullah ibn Jud'an to buy new sandals and new garments when he buys, to seek a noble, spirited mount, and to honour a noblewoman entrusted to him. > **""When you buy sandals, buy them new. When you buy a garment, buy it new. When you buy a mount, seek a choice one. When a noblewoman of a people is with you, honour her.""**"
"6530","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counselled 'Amr ibn Jud'an to choose new footwear and new clothing when he purchases, preferring sound, unworn quality. > **""When you buy sandals, buy them new, and when you buy a garment, buy it new.""**"
"6531","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, carried on a horse in the path of Allah, then found one of its offspring offered for sale, and was forbidden to buy it back, lest charity return to its giver by purchase. > **""Do not buy back what you gave in the path of Allah.""**"
"6532","'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he was given a she-camel in the path of Allah and wished to buy from its progeny. He asked the Prophet ﷺ, who bade him leave it, so that it and all its offspring would come in his scale on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Leave her; she and all her offspring will come in your scale on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6533","Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he gave a mare of his in charity, then saw her filly stood up for sale in the market and wished to buy her. He came to the Prophet ﷺ to ask concerning it, scrupling to take back his charity by purchase. > **""I gave a mare in charity and saw her daughter offered in the market — may I buy her?""**"
"6534","وَفِي رِوَايَةٍ عَنْ زَيْدِ بْنِ حَارِثَةَ أَيْضًا قَالَ: حَمَلْتُ عَلَى فَرَسِي فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ، وَإِنِّي رَأَيْتُهُ بَعْدُ يُبَاعُ فِي السُّوقِ بِثَمَنٍ يَسِيرٍ مَهْزُولٍ مَضْرُوبٍ، وَقَدْ عَرَفْتُ عُرْفَهُ. In another narration Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he had given his horse in the path of Allah, then later saw it sold in the market for a paltry price, emaciated and ill-kept, recognising it by its forelock, and raised the matter to the Prophet ﷺ. > **""I carried on my horse in the path of Allah, then saw it sold cheap in the market, lean and beaten, and I knew its forelock.""**"
"6535","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bought a newly arrived caravan, gained some ounces of gold as profit, and gave them in charity to the widows of Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib, declaring his principle never to buy what he could not pay for. > **""I do not buy anything for which I do not have its price with me.""**"
"6536","Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established the comprehensive legal maxim that harm is neither to be inflicted nor to be reciprocated in Islam. > **""There is no harm and no reciprocal harm in Islam.""**"
"6537","وَعَنْ عَائِشَةَ أَنَّ رَسُولَ اللَّهِ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ - قَالَ: «لَا ضَرَرَ، وَلَا ضِرَارَ». 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there is to be neither infliction of harm nor its reciprocation, a foundation for compensation and prevention of injury in transactions and neighbourly rights. > **""There is no harm and no reciprocal harm.""**"
"6538","Abu Shurayh, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that one who graciously rescinds a sale at his brother's regretful request — iqalah — will have his own slips cancelled by Allah on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever rescinds a sale for his brother, Allah will cancel his slip on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6539","'Amr ibn Hurayth reports that when he came to Madinah and divided property with his brother, Sa'id ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, conveyed that the price of land or a house carries no blessing unless it is placed back into land or a house. > **""There is no blessing in the price of land or a house which is not placed into land or a house.""**"
"6540","وَعَنْ رَجُلٍ مِنَ الْحَيِّ «أَنَّ يَعْلَى بْنَ سُهَيْلٍ مَرَّ بِعِمْرَانَ بْنِ حُصَيْنٍ، فَقَالَ لَهُ: يَا يَعْلَى، أَلَمْ أُنَبَّأْ أَنَّكَ بِعْتَ دَارًا بِمِائَةِ أَلْفٍ؟ قَالَ: بَلَى قَدْ بِعْتُهَا بِمِائَةِ أَلْفٍ! قَالَ: فَإِنِّي سَمِعْتُ رَسُولَ اللَّهِ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ - يَقُولُ: «مَنْ بَاعَ عُقْدَةَ مَالٍ سَلَّطَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ تَالِفًا يُتْلِفُهَا». A tribesman reports that Ya'la ibn Suhayl passed by 'Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, who had heard he sold a house for a hundred thousand. 'Imran warned him that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that one who sells a knotted holding of wealth has a destroyer set over it to squander it. > **""Whoever sells a knotted holding of wealth, Allah sets over it a destroyer that destroys it.""**"
"6541","وَعَنْ عِمْرَانَ بْنِ حُصَيْنٍ قَالَ: قَالَ رَسُولُ اللَّهِ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ -: «مَا مِنْ عَبْدٍ يَبِيعُ تَالِدًا إِلَّا سَلَّطَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ تَالِفًا». 'Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said no servant sells inherited, long-held property but that Allah sets a cause of ruin over its price to consume it. > **""There is no servant who sells inherited property except that Allah sets a destroyer over him.""**"
"6542","وَعَنْ حُذَيْفَةَ، وَعَمْرِو بْنِ حُرَيْثٍ قَالَا: قَالَ رَسُولُ اللَّهِ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ -: «مَنْ بَاعَ دَارًا، وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ ثَمَنَهَا فِي مِثْلِهَا لَمْ يُبَارَكْ لَهُ فِيهِ». Hudhayfah and 'Amr ibn Hurayth, may Allah be pleased with them, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said one who sells a house and fails to place its price into another like it will find no blessing in that money. > **""Whoever sells a house and does not place its price into its like, it will not be blessed for him.""**"
"6543","'Abdullah ibn Ya'la al-Laythi, judge of Basrah, reports that Ma'qil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, sold a house for a hundred thousand and cited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: any man who sells landed estate without need has a destroyer sent by Allah to squander it. > **""Any man who sells landed estate without need, Allah sends a destroyer to destroy it.""**"
"6544","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said one who sells a house without acquiring another in its stead will have no blessing placed in its price. > **""Whoever sells a house and does not replace it, its price will not be blessed for him.""**"
"6545","Al-Sha'bi reports that 'Utbah ibn Farqad bought land on the bank of the Euphrates and built upon it. When 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came, he asked from whom it was bought. 'Utbah claimed its owners, but in the evening gathering the actual proprietors denied selling anything. 'Umar ruled that these were its true owners, ordering the land returned to the seller and the price reclaimed."
"6546","'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, asked Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, about the Prophet's ﷺ ruling on the lands of the non-Arabs. He answered that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling them, buying them, and leasing them, guarding the kharaj trust from private appropriation. > **""The Messenger of Allah forbade selling the lands of the non-Arabs, buying them, and leasing them.""**"
"6547","وَعَنْ عَاصِمِ بْنِ عَدِيٍّ قَالَ: اشْتَرَيْتُ أَنَا، وَأَخِي مِائَةَ سَهْمٍ مِنْ سِهَامِ خَيْبَرَ، فَبَلَغَ ذَلِكَ النَّبِيَّ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ - فَقَالَ: «يَا عَاصِمُ، مَا ذِئْبَانِ عَادِيَانِ أَصَابَا غَنَمًا أَضَاعَهَا رَبُّهَا بِأَفْسَدَ لَهَا مِنْ حُبِّ الْمَرْءِ الْمَالَ وَالسَّرَفَ لِدِينِهِ». 'Asim ibn 'Adi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he and his brother bought a hundred shares of Khaybar. When it reached the Prophet ﷺ, he warned that two raiding wolves set upon neglected sheep corrupt them less than a man's love of wealth and eminence corrupts his religion. > **""O 'Asim, two raiding wolves that seize sheep whose owner neglected them corrupt them no more than a man's love of wealth and honour corrupts his religion.""**"
"6548","Muhammad ibn Suqah reports from his father that when 'Amr ibn Hurayth built his house, the narrator came to rent a shop for trade. 'Amr told him the house is blessed for dweller, buyer and seller, for the Prophet ﷺ had once scooped dirhams with his own hand and given them to him. He entrusted them to his mother, later mixed them into the purchase price of the Kufan house, confident in their blessing. > **""Here, O 'Amr, take these dirhams. This house is blessed for whoever dwells in it, blessed for whoever buys and sells in it.""**"
"6549","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when guests came to the Prophet ﷺ, Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, exchanged two sa' of their inferior dates for one sa' of fine dates. The Prophet ﷺ admired the dates, asked their source, and when told, ordered the transaction reversed as riba. > **""Whence is this? Return our dates to us.""**"
"6550","Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he sold his dates for finer dates at half their measure and presented them to the Prophet ﷺ. Ordered to return them, take back his own dates, sell them for wheat or barley and then buy the fine dates, he did so. The Prophet ﷺ then laid down the rule of equality in same-genus exchange and lawfulness of disparity when genera differ. > **""Go, return it to its owner, take back your dates, sell them for wheat or barley, then buy of these dates. Dates for dates equal for equal, wheat for wheat equal for equal, barley for barley equal for equal, salt for salt equal for equal, silver for silver weight for weight. Whatever is in excess is riba; but when the two types differ there is no harm, even one for ten.""**"
"6551","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Rayyan dates were brought to the Prophet ﷺ. Told they were obtained by selling two sa' of Ba'l dates for one, he ordered them returned to their owner, and in one narration instructed them to sell their own dates for coined money first and then buy the desired dates. > **""Return it to its owner. Sell yours for money, then buy the dates.""**"
"6552","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ desired dates and was brought a sa' of 'Ajwah. Finding it strange, he asked its source. Told it was got by selling two sa' for one, he twice ordered it returned, declaring no need of it. > **""Return it, return it; we have no need of it.""**"
"6553","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports the same prohibition of selling one dinar for two, one dirham for two, and one sa' for two from fear of riba, with the same exception that horses for horses and a noble she-camel for camels entail no harm when hand to hand. > **""Do not sell one dinar for two dinars, nor one dirham for two dirhams, nor one sa' for two sa's. I fear al-rama' for you — and al-rama' is riba. There is no harm when it is hand to hand.""**"
"6554","Ibn 'Umar, Abu Sa'id and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, report that the Prophet ﷺ required gold for gold and silver for silver equal for equal, spot for coined spot. Whoever gives more or demands more commits riba. > **""Gold for gold equal for equal, silver for silver equal for equal, spot for spot. Whoever gives more or takes more has committed riba.""**"
"6555","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ listed gold, silver, wheat, barley, salt and dates, requiring equality and equal measure in same-genus exchange. Giving or demanding increase is riba. > **""Gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, salt for salt, dates for dates — equal for equal, measure for measure. Whoever gives more or seeks more has committed riba.""**"
"6556","وَعَنْ أَبِي الزُّبَيْرِ الْمَكِّيِّ قَالَ: سَأَلْتُ جَابِرَ بْنَ عَبْدِ اللَّهِ، عَنِ الْحِنْطَةِ بِالتَّمْرِ بِفَضْلٍ يَدًا بِيَدٍ؟ فَقَالَ: «قَدْ كُنَّا عَلَى عَهْدِ رَسُولِ اللَّهِ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ - نَشْتَرِي الصَّاعَ الْحِنْطَةَ بِسِتِّ آصُعٍ مِنْ تَمْرٍ يَدًا بِيَدٍ. فَإِنْ كَانَ نَوْعًا وَاحِدًا فَلَا خَيْرَ فِيهِ إِلَّا مِثْلًا بِمِثْلٍ». Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki asked Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, about wheat for dates with disparity, hand to hand. Jabir answered that in the Prophet's ﷺ time they bought one sa' of wheat for six sa' of dates on the spot, but if the genus is one, there is no good in it except equal for equal."
"6557","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports the rule of equality in gold, silver, wheat, barley and salt. When told that the keeper of the Prophet's ﷺ dates bought one sa' for two, the keeper pleaded that his poor dates were taken only with increase. The Prophet ﷺ forbade him outright. > **""Gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, salt for salt — equal for equal. Whoever gives more and takes more has committed riba. Do not do it.""**"
"6558","Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki reports that Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, had permitted one dinar for two by personal reasoning. Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi, may Allah be pleased with him, rebuked him sternly and testified that he heard the Prophet ﷺ require equality without excess in dinar, dirham, wheat, barley and salt. Ibn 'Abbas withdrew, admitting he had spoken from opinion without a heard text. > **""Dinar for dinar, dirham for dirham, a sa' of wheat for a sa' of wheat, a sa' of barley for a sa' of barley, a sa' of salt for a sa' of salt — with no excess between any of that.""**"
"6559","عَنْ جَابِرِ بْنِ عَبْدِ اللَّهِ، وَأَبِي سَعِيدٍ، وَأَبِي هُرَيْرَةَ أَنَّهُمْ نَهَوْا عَنِ الصَّرْفِ. رَفَعَهُ رَجُلَانِ مِنْهُمْ إِلَى النَّبِيِّ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ -. Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, Abu Sa'id and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, all forbade sarf — currency exchange with delay or disparity — and two of them transmitted the prohibition as raised to the Prophet ﷺ himself. > **""The Prophet forbade sarf except hand to hand, equal for equal.""**"
"6560","Abu Qilabah reports that people bought gold for silver on credit till the state stipend. Hisham ibn 'Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, passed them, forbade them, and informed them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling gold for silver on credit — and that this is riba. > **""The Messenger of Allah forbade us to sell gold for silver on credit; that is riba.""**"
"6561","Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, went out to sell two anklets for household expenses and met Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, who sought finer silver for his dirhams. Weighed, the anklets outweighed the dirhams slightly, so Abu Bakr called for shears to cut the excess. When Abu Rafi' waived it, Abu Bakr refused, citing the Prophet ﷺ that excess giver and taker in gold and silver are in the Fire. > **""Gold for gold equal for equal, silver for silver equal for equal. The giver of excess and the taker of excess are in the Fire.""**"
"6562","Shurahbil ibn Sa'd reports from Ibn 'Umar, Abu Hurayrah and Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Prophet ﷺ required gold for gold and silver for silver equal for equal, spot for spot, with increase deemed riba. Shurahbil sealed it with an oath invoking the Fire upon himself if he had not heard it. > **""Gold for gold equal for equal, silver for silver equal for equal, spot for spot. Whoever gives more or takes more has committed riba.""**"
"6563","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether he might buy gold with silver and silver with gold. He ﷺ permitted it on condition that the two do not part while any confusion or unsettled claim remains between them — immediate, clear possession. > **""When you buy one of them with the other, let your companion not leave you while any ambiguity remains between you and him.""**"
"6564","Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, who worked as a goldsmith for the wives of the Prophet ﷺ, reports that they told him they heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ require gold for gold and silver for silver weight for weight, with increase deemed riba. > **""Gold for gold and silver for silver, weight for weight. Whoever gives more or seeks more has committed riba.""**"
"6565","Anas and 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with them, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ required gold for gold and silver for silver equal for equal. > **""Gold for gold equal for equal, and silver for silver equal for equal.""**"
"6566","Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade sarf — unequal or deferred currency exchange — two months before his passing ﷺ, confirming the ruling stood firm to the end of his life. > **""The Prophet forbade sarf two months before his death.""**"
"6567","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, declared that gold is for gold weight for weight, and giving or taking excess is riba. The transmitter swears by Allah that Ibn 'Umar never lied against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"6568","Bishr ibn Harb asked Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, whether he might take one dirham for two. He answered that this is riba itself, not to be approached, citing the Prophet's ﷺ command to take equal for equal. > **""Take equal for equal.""**"
"6569","Abu al-Ma'arik reports that in 'Uthman's time, may Allah be pleased with him, a Ghafiq man was owed a hundred deferred dinars by a Mahrah man. After spoils arrived, the debtor offered seventy promptly to extinguish the hundred, and the creditor agreed. Al-Miqdad, may Allah be pleased with him, seized his mount's bridle and, hearing the tale, declared both had announced war from Allah and His Messenger ﷺ by this riba of settlement."
"6570","Sa'd ibn Iyas reports that 'Abdullah — Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him — once permitted one dirham for two and one dinar for two. After journeying to Madinah and meeting 'Umar, 'Ali and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with them, who forbade him, he returned circling the money-changers warning the people neither to consume riba nor to buy one dirham for two or one dinar for two."
"6571","Abu Hurrah al-Raqashi reports from his uncle, who held the halter of the Prophet's ﷺ she-camel on the middle day of Tashriq in the Farewell Hajj. Among his words ﷺ that day was the abolition of all riba, beginning with the riba of al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, with capitals retained and neither wronging nor wronged. > **""O people, all riba is abolished. The first riba abolished is the riba of al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib. You shall have your capitals; you shall neither wrong nor be wronged.""**"
"6572","وَعَنْ عَبْدِ اللَّهِ - يَعْنِي ابْنَ مَسْعُودٍ - أَنَّ النَّبِيَّ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ - قَالَ: «الرِّبَا سَبْعُونَ بَابًا، وَالشِّرْكُ مِثْلُ ذَلِكَ». 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said riba has seventy doors, and shirk is the like thereof in manifold grades. > **""Riba has seventy doors, and shirk is the like thereof.""**"
"6573","'Abdullah ibn Hanzalah, washed by the angels, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said a single dirham of riba eaten knowingly is more severe than thirty-six acts of adultery. > **""A dirham of riba which a man eats knowingly is worse than thirty-six adulteries.""**"
"6574","'Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said a dirham a man acquires through riba is greater before Allah than thirty-three adulteries committed in Islam. > **""A dirham a man acquires from riba is greater before Allah than thirty-three adulteries he commits in Islam.""**"
"6575","Al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said riba has seventy-two doors, the least like a man approaching his own mother, and the greatest riba is a man's prolonged assault upon his brother's honour. > **""Riba has seventy-two doors, the least of them like a man approaching his mother. The greatest riba is a man's aggression against his brother's honour.""**"
"6576","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that aiding an oppressor with falsehood to defeat truth severs one from Allah's and His Messenger's protection, that eating a dirham of riba equals thirty-three adulteries, and that flesh nourished on the unlawful is most deserving of the Fire. > **""Whoever aids an oppressor with falsehood to refute truth with it is free of Allah's protection and His Messenger's protection. Whoever eats a dirham of riba is like thirty-three adulteries, and whatever flesh grows of the unlawful, the Fire is most worthy of it.""**"
"6577","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that on the night he ﷺ was taken aloft, when they reached the seventh heaven amid thunder, lightning and thunderbolts, he passed people whose bellies were like houses containing snakes visible from outside. Jibril, peace be upon him, identified them as the eaters of riba. > **""I came upon people whose bellies were like houses, with snakes inside them visible from outside their bellies. I said: O Jibril, who are these? He said: These are the eaters of riba.""**"
"6578","Ka'b al-Ahbar declares that committing adultery thirty-three times is dearer to him — i.e. lighter in his sight — than eating a single dirham of riba while Allah knows he ate it as riba, magnifying the enormity of conscious usury."
"6579","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, declares that the eater of riba, its giver, its scribe and its two witnesses when they know of it, the tattooing woman and the woman tattooed for beautification, the withholder of sadaqah, and the apostate who returns to Bedouin life after Hijrah are all cursed upon the tongue of Muhammad ﷺ. > **""The eater of riba, its giver, its scribe and its two witnesses when they know of it are cursed upon the tongue of Muhammad.""**"
"6580","'Amr ibn al-'As, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say no people among whom riba becomes manifest escape seizure by drought and years of famine, and no people among whom bribes spread escape seizure by terror. > **""There are no people among whom riba appears except they are seized by famine, and no people among whom bribes appear except they are seized by terror.""**"
"6581","Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that whenever adultery and riba become manifest among a people, they render Allah's punishment lawful upon themselves. > **""Whenever zina and riba appear among a people, they make Allah's punishment lawful upon themselves.""**"
"6582","وَعَنِ ابْنِ مَسْعُودٍ، عَنِ النَّبِيِّ - صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ - قَالَ: «بَيْنَ يَدَيِ السَّاعَةِ يَظْهَرُ الرِّبَا، وَالزِّنَا، وَالْخَمْرُ». Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ foretold that before the Hour, riba, adultery and wine will become openly manifest. > **""Before the Hour, riba, zina and wine will appear.""**"
"6583","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said when adultery and riba appear in a township, its people render the Book of Allah — His decreed punishment — lawful upon themselves. > **""When zina and riba appear in a village, they make the decree of Allah Almighty lawful upon themselves.""**"
"6584","وَعَنْ عَبْدِ اللَّهِ بْنِ مَسْعُودٍ قَالَ: لَمْ يُهْلَكْ أَهْلُ بَلْدَةٍ قَطُّ حَتَّى يَظْهَرَ فِيهِمُ الرِّبَا، وَالزِّنَا. 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, declares that the people of no town were ever destroyed until riba and adultery became manifest among them."
"6585","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning riba. > **""The Prophet ﷺ cursed the eater of riba and its feeder.""**"
"6586","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning those who knowingly participate in riba. > **""The Prophet ﷺ cursed the eater of riba and its feeder, its scribe and its witness, while they know.""**"
"6587","Al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Wahid al-Wazzan said: I saw Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa (رضي الله عنه) in the market among the money-changers. He said: O company of money-changers, receive glad tidings. They said: May Allah gladden you with Paradise, O Abu Muhammad, with what do you gladden us? He informed them of the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Receive glad tidings of the Fire.""**"
"6588","Awf ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, warning against sins that are not forgiven, explaining the state of the traitor and the eater of riba on the Day of Resurrection, then reciting the verse of Surah al-Baqarah. > **""Beware of the sins that are not forgiven: misappropriation of booty, for whoever misappropriates anything will come with it on the Day of Resurrection; and eating riba, for whoever eats riba will come on the Day of Resurrection insane, staggering. Then he recited: Those who eat riba do not stand except as stands one whom Satan has confounded with touch.""**"
"6589","Abdullah ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) explained the verse, Those who eat riba do not stand except as stands one whom Satan has confounded with touch, saying they will be recognized by that on the Day of Resurrection as standing only like the insane strangled one, and explained His saying, That is because they said trade is like riba, that they lied against Allah, while Allah permitted trade and forbade riba, and whoever receives admonition from his Lord and desists, up to His saying, and whoever returns and eats riba, then those are companions of the Fire abiding therein, and explained His saying, O you who believe, fear Allah and leave what remains of riba if you are believers, but if you do not, then take notice of war from Allah and His Messenger, to the end of the verse. It reached us, and Allah knows best, that this verse was revealed concerning Banu Amr ibn Umayr ibn Awf of Thaqif and Banu al-Mughirah of Makhzum, for Banu al-Mughirah used to take riba from Thaqif, then when Allah gave His Messenger ﷺ victory over Makkah he abolished all riba that day. The people of Taif had made treaty that their riba claims remained while what was upon them of riba was abolished, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote at the end of their document that they have what Muslims have and upon them is what is upon Muslims, that they shall not eat riba nor feed it. Then Banu Amr ibn Umayr and Banu al-Mughirah came to Attab ibn Asid (رضي الله عنه), who was over Makkah. Banu al-Mughirah said: Has riba made us the most wretched of people, abolished from people other than us? Banu Amr ibn Umayr said: We were treatied that our riba remains. So Attab ibn Asid wrote concerning that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and this verse was revealed."
"6590","Tariq ibn Shihab (رحمه الله) said: We used to sell the ornamented sword and buy silver in exchange for it."
"6591","The daughter of Utbah ibn Ulaylah and a woman of the family of Abu Umamah reported that they both heard Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, concerning the household upon whom agricultural implements come in the morning. > **""There is no household upon whom a faddan comes in the morning except they are humiliated.""**"
"6592","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by land of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) in which there was cultivation, and addressed him as Abu Abd al-Rahman. > **""O Abu Abd al-Rahman, do not eat riba nor feed it, and do not cultivate except in land you inherit, or bequeath, or grant.""**"
"6593","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning speech in cultivation. > **""None of you should say: I sowed; but let him say: I ploughed.""**"
"6594","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that Allah granted Khaybar as fay to His Messenger ﷺ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left its people there and placed it between him and them. He sent Abdullah ibn Rawahah (رضي الله عنه), who assessed its produce upon them, then addressed the Jews concerning his hatred of them for killing prophets of Allah and lying against Allah Almighty, that his hatred would not carry him to wrong them, that he had assessed twenty thousand wasqs of dates, giving them choice to take or leave. > **""O company of Jews, you are the most hateful of people to me; you killed the prophets of Allah and lied against Allah Almighty, yet my hatred of you does not carry me to wrong you. I have assessed twenty thousand wasqs of dates; if you wish, it is yours, and if you refuse, it is mine. They said: By this the heavens and the earth stand.""**"
"6595","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ sent Ibn Rawahah (رضي الله عنه) to Khaybar to assess upon them, then gave them choice to take or return, whereupon they affirmed justice. > **""Ibn Rawahah assessed upon them, then gave them choice to take or return, and they said: By this the heavens and the earth stand.""**"
"6596","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Khaybar he promised the Jews to give them half the fruits on condition they cultivate it, saying he would leave them as long as Allah left them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to send Abdullah ibn Rawahah (رضي الله عنه) to assess it, then give them choice to take or leave. The Jews came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining of the severity of his assessment, so he called Abdullah ibn Rawahah and mentioned what they said. Abdullah said: This is what I have, O Messenger of Allah; if they wish they take it, and if they wish they leave it and we take it. So the Jews were satisfied and said: By this the heavens and the earth stand. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said in the illness in which he died, and Umar (رضي الله عنه) later conveyed his treaty condition and expelled every Jew and Christian from the land of Hijaz, then divided it among the people of Madinah. > **""I will leave you as long as Allah leaves you. Two religions shall not coexist in the Arabian Peninsula.""**"
"6597","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted Khaybar for a share of its produce. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted Khaybar upon half or upon a third.""**"
"6598","Urwah (رحمه الله) reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ opened Khaybar he sent Abdullah ibn Rawahah (رضي الله عنه) to apportion with the Jews. When he came to them they began offering him food as gifts, so he disliked taking anything from them and said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had sent him as a just arbiter between him and them and he had no need of their gifts. He assessed the palms, and when he established the assessment Abdullah gave them choice: if they wished he would guarantee their share while they cultivated, and if they wished they would guarantee the Muslim share while they cultivated. So they chose to guarantee and cultivate, and said: O Ibn Rawahah, this which you present to us and act upon today, by it the heavens and the earth stand, for they stand only by truth. Khaybar belonged to those who witnessed Hudaybiyah, none else shared with them, none of them stayed behind, none other witnessed it, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not permit anyone who stayed behind from his expedition to Hudaybiyah to witness Khaybar."
"6599","Ibn Shihab (رحمه الله) reported concerning the conquest of Khaybar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Abdullah ibn Rawahah (رضي الله عنه) to apportion its fruits with the Jews. When he came to them they began offering him food, speaking to him, and assigning for him jewelry from their women's jewelry, saying: This is yours, lighten for us and overlook. Ibn Rawahah said: O company of Jews, by Allah you are the most hateful of people to me, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me only as a just arbiter between you and him, I have no desire for your worldly goods and I will not wrong you; you have only offered me unlawful gain which I will not eat. He assessed the palms, and when he established the assessment he gave them choice: if they wished he would guarantee their share, and if they wished they would guarantee the Muslim share and cultivate. So they chose to guarantee and cultivate, saying: O Ibn Rawahah, this which you act upon, by it the heavens and the earth stand, for they stand only by truth."
"6600","Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr (رحمه الله) reported concerning the settlement of the Prophet ﷺ with the Jews of Khaybar that half the dates would be for the Muslims and half for the Jews while the Jews sufficed the labour, until when their fruits ripened they came to the Prophet ﷺ and said their dates had ripened, so send an assessor to assess between us and you. So the Prophet ﷺ sent Abdullah ibn Rawahah (رضي الله عنه). When he toured their palms and looked at them he declared their fabrication against Allah and enmity to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that none was more hateful to him than them, yet that would not carry him to wrong them even an atom's weight. He then assessed all of it, what belonged to the Muslims and what belonged to the Jews, as eighty thousand wasqs. The Jews said: You have wronged us. Ibn Rawahah said: If you wish, give us forty thousand wasqs and we hand over the fruits to you, and if you wish, we give you forty thousand wasqs and you separate from us. They looked at one another, then said: By this the heavens and the earth stand, and by this you overcome them."
"6601","Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to the villages of Uraynah and commanded him to take the share of the land, which al-Ashjai explained as meaning a third and a quarter. > **""Take the share of the land.""**"
"6602","Rafi ibn Khadij (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade muhaqalah and muzabanah, explaining that only three cultivate. > **""Only three cultivate: a man who has land so he cultivates it, a man who is granted land so he cultivates, and a man who rents land for gold or silver.""**"
"6603","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ did not forbid renting land but commanded noble morals. > **""The Prophet ﷺ did not forbid renting land, but he commanded noble morals.""**"
"6604","Amir ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nistas reported concerning the conquest of Khaybar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ opened it and all of it, its tilth and its palms, belonged to him, while the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions had no helpers, so the Prophet ﷺ reconciled with the Jews that they would suffice the labour with half the dates for them, on condition he would leave them as long as seemed fit to Allah and His Messenger. That is when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Ibn Rawahah (رضي الله عنه) to assess between them, and when he gave them choice the Jews took the dates. Khaybar remained thereafter with the Jews upon the treaty of the Prophet ﷺ until Umar (رضي الله عنه) expelled them, when the Jews said: Did the Prophet ﷺ not reconcile with us for such and such? He said: Yes, on condition to leave you as long as seemed fit to Allah and His Messenger, and now it seems fit to me to expel you. So he expelled them, then divided it among the Muslims who had conquered it with the Prophet ﷺ, giving none who was not present at its conquest. Its people today are Muslims with no Jew therein. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded assessment only to count the zakah before the fruits were eaten."
"6605","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that a woman entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and said: By my father and mother, I and my son bought from so-and-so the dates of his property, we counted and gathered them, yet by Him Who honoured you with what He honoured you, we obtained nothing except what we eat in our stomachs or feed a poor person hoping for blessing. So we sent to him and came seeking remission of our loss, but he swore by Allah he would remit nothing. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it three times, until news reached the owner of the dates and he came, saying: By my father and mother, if you wish I will remit what they lost, and if you wish, from the principal, as you wish. So he remitted for them what they had lost. > **""Will he not do good?""**"
"6606","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) wrote to his agent over land not to withhold surplus water, for he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever withholds surplus water to withhold thereby surplus herbage, Allah will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6607","In another narration from Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning withholding surplus. > **""Whoever withholds his surplus water or surplus herbage.""**"
"6608","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, warning against withholding surplus water and herbage lest water diminish and dependents hunger. > **""Do not withhold surplus water, nor withhold herbage, lest water dwindle and dependents hunger.""**"
"6609","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه), with al-Mas'udi doubting only whether he raised it, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning surplus water and pasture after need is met. > **""Do not withhold surplus water after you are sufficed from it, nor surplus pasture.""**"
"6610","Sa'd, meaning Ibn Abi Waqqas (رضي الله عنه), reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever withholds surplus water, Allah will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6611","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Two qualities, it is not lawful to withhold them: water and fire.""**"
"6612","Abdullah ibn Sarjis (رضي الله عنه) reported: I came to the Prophet ﷺ and entered between his shirt and his skin, kissing the place of the seal, then said: What is it that is not lawful to withhold? He said: Salt. I said: Then what? He said: > **""Water and fire.""**"
"6613","Wathilah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not withhold from the servants of Allah surplus water, nor herbage, nor fire, for Allah the Exalted has made them provision for the traveller and strength for the weak.""**"
"6614","Samurah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say concerning the road, surplus water, the traveller's loan of bucket, rope and basin, access to the well, and the space around a dug well. > **""No road is cut, nor surplus water withheld, nor the traveller denied loan of bucket, rope and basin if he has no tools to help him, and he is left between him and the well to water, nor digging prevented when the digger leaves twenty-five cubits as resting place for his livestock.""**"
"6615","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Any man whose cousin comes to him asking from his surplus and he withholds it, Allah will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever withholds surplus water to withhold thereby surplus herbage, Allah will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6616","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The sanctuary of the well is forty cubits around it, all of it for the resting of camels and sheep, and the traveller is the first to drink, and surplus water is not withheld to withhold thereby herbage.""**"
"6617","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to a Christian trader to send garments on credit till ease. He came to him saying the Messenger of Allah sent him for garments till ease, but the man asked: What is ease? When is ease? By Allah, Muhammad has neither bleating livestock nor grazing livestock. So he returned to the Prophet ﷺ, and when the Prophet ﷺ saw him he declared the enemy of Allah a liar and that he himself was best of those who trade, and that patched garments were better than betraying trust. > **""The enemy of Allah has lied; I am the best of those who trade. For one of you to wear a garment of diverse patches is better for him than to take in his trust what he does not have.""**"
"6618","Anas (رضي الله عنه), in the narration of al-Tabarani in al-Awsat and al-Bazzar like it except that he is one who has neither crop nor udder, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to the Jews to borrow till ease. A man said: What ease has he? He is one who has neither root nor branch. So he returned to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, whereupon he declared the enemy of Allah a liar and affirmed payment had he been given. > **""The enemy of Allah has lied; had he given us, we would have paid him.""**"
"6619","Abu Rafi (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hosted a guest but found with the Prophet ﷺ nothing to prepare for him, so he sent to a man of the Jews saying: Muhammad the Messenger of Allah says to you: Lend me flour till the crescent of Rajab. He said: No, except with pledge. So the narrator came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him. He said: By Allah, I am trustworthy in heaven, trustworthy on earth; had he lent me or sold to me I would have paid him. When he left him this verse was revealed: And do not extend your eyes to what We have given pairs of them, to the end of the verse, diverting him from the world. > **""By Allah, I am trustworthy in heaven, trustworthy on earth; had he lent me or sold to me, I would have paid him.""**"
"6620","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""It is not befitting a servant to come to his brother asking him a loan while he has means, then he withholds it from him.""**"
"6621","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""For every loan is charity.""**"
"6622","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that a man entered Paradise and saw written upon its gate the reward of charity and loan. > **""A man entered Paradise and saw written upon its gate: Charity is tenfold, and loan is eighteenfold.""**"
"6623","Uqbah ibn Amir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: Do not frighten yourselves after your security. They said: What is that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: > **""Debt.""**"
"6624","From Uqbah also (رضي الله عنه) that the Prophet ﷺ said to his Companions: Do not frighten yourselves, or he said: souls. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, with what do we frighten ourselves? He said: > **""Debt.""**"
"6625","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man died, so they washed, shrouded and embalmed him, then brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to pray over him. He stepped forward, then asked about debt upon him. When told two dinars, he turned away until Abu Qatadah (رضي الله عنه) assumed them. They came to him, and Abu Qatadah said: The two dinars are upon me. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether Allah had fulfilled the creditor's right and the deceased was freed therefrom. He said: Yes. So he prayed over him, then after a day asked what became of the two dinars. When told he had died only yesterday, he returned the next day and said he had settled them. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Has Allah fulfilled the creditor's right and the deceased is freed therefrom? Now his skin has cooled upon him.""**"
"6626","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought a funeral, so he stood to pray over it. They said debt was upon him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Take your companion and pray over him. A man said: His debt is upon me, so pray over him. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and prayed over him. > **""Take your companion and pray over him.""**"
"6627","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: What if I strive with myself and my wealth, killed patient, seeking reward, advancing not retreating, shall I enter Paradise? He said: Yes. He repeated it twice or thrice, and he said: Yes, if there is no debt upon you for which you have no repayment. > **""Yes, if there is no debt upon you for which you have no repayment.""**"
"6628","Muhammad ibn Jahsh (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, what is for me if I fight in the path of Allah till I am killed? He said: Paradise. When he turned away, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Paradise, except debt; Jibril (عليه السلام) just whispered it to me.""**"
"6629","Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Jahsh, from his father (رضي الله عنهما), reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, if I am killed in the path of Allah? He said: Paradise. When he turned away he said: > **""Paradise. Debt was whispered to me by Jibril (عليه السلام) just now.""**"
"6630","Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Jahsh (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Had a man been killed in the path of Allah, then revived, then killed in the path of Allah, then revived, he would not enter Paradise until his debt is settled for him, for there is neither gold nor silver there; there are only good deeds and evil deeds.""**"
"6631","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and preached, mentioning belief in Allah and jihad in the path of Allah among the best deeds before Allah. A man stood and asked: O Messenger of Allah, if I am killed in the path of Allah, patient, seeking reward, advancing not retreating, will Allah expiate my sins? He said: Yes. He asked how he had spoken, repeating the question upon him as he had said, and he again said: Yes, then asked again, until the man repeated: O Messenger of Allah, if I am killed in the path of Allah patient, seeking reward, advancing not retreating, will Allah expiate my sins? He said: Yes, except debt, for Jibril whispered that to me. > **""Yes, except debt, for Jibril whispered that to me.""**"
"6632","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the dawn prayer, then said, asking for one of Hudhayl, that their companion was detained at the gate of Paradise, he thought he said: due to his debt. > **""Is anyone here from Hudhayl? Your companion is detained at the gate of Paradise due to his debt.""**"
"6633","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, a man fights in the path of Allah seeking reward till he is killed, is he in Paradise? He said: Yes. When he turned away he called him and said that Jibril (عليه السلام) had informed him of the condition of debt. > **""Yes. Jibril (عليه السلام) came to me and said: If there is no debt upon him.""**"
"6634","Sahl ibn Hunayf (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""With the first shedding of the martyr's blood, all his sins are forgiven except debt.""**"
"6635","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Heedlessness is in three: from remembrance of Allah, and when the dawn prayer is prayed until the sun rises, and a man's heedlessness of himself until debt mounts him.""**"
"6636","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, then turned and asked whether anyone from Banu so-and-so was present. None answered him. He repeated it a second and third time until a man said: I am here, O Messenger of Allah. He asked what prevented him from standing. He said he feared something new had occurred. He denied that, informing that their companion so-and-so had been detained at the gate of Paradise due to his debt. The man said: His debt is upon me, O Messenger of Allah. > **""Is anyone here from Banu so-and-so? Your companion so-and-so has been detained at the gate of Paradise due to his debt.""**"
"6637","Sa'd ibn al-Atwal (رضي الله عنه) reported that his father died leaving three hundred dirhams, dependents and debt, so he wished to spend upon his dependents. The Prophet ﷺ informed him his father was detained due to his debt and commanded him to settle for him. He said: By my father and mother, O Messenger of Allah, I have settled all except a woman who claimed two dinars without proof. He said: Give her, for she is truthful. So he gave her. > **""Your father is detained due to his debt, so settle for him. Give her, for she is truthful.""**"
"6638","A man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reported the like of the previous narration concerning settling debt for the deceased father."
"6639","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, and when he finished asked whether anyone from Banu so-and-so was present. None answered. He repeated it again and a third time until a man said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, here is so-and-so. He informed that their companion was detained at the gate of Paradise due to debt upon him. A man said: His debt is upon me, O Messenger of Allah. > **""Is anyone here from Banu so-and-so? Your companion is detained at the gate of Paradise due to debt upon him.""**"
"6640","Al-Bara ibn Azib (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""The debtor is held captive by his debt, complaining to Allah of solitude.""**"
"6641","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no worry except the worry of debt, and no pain except the pain of the eye.""**"
"6642","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, the Hajj of Islam is upon me, and debt is upon me. He said: > **""Settle your debt.""**"
"6643","Abu Hadrad al-Aslami (رضي الله عنه) owed a Jew four dirhams, so the Jew sought redress, saying: O Muhammad, this one owes me four dirhams and has overpowered me concerning them. He said: Give him his right. He said: By Him Who sent you with truth, I cannot afford them. He said: Give him his right. He said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I cannot afford them. He informed him that he had told the Jew the Prophet would send them to Khaybar, hoping they would gain something and returning he would pay his right. He again said: Give him his right. When the Prophet ﷺ said a matter thrice it was not reviewed. So Ibn Abi Hadrad took him to the market with a band on his head and wrapped in a cloak as waist-wrapper, removed the turban from his head to wrap with it, removed the cloak and offered it for sale for the dirhams. An old woman passed and asked what was wrong, O Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He informed her, so she gave him both cloak and payment, casting upon him the cloak he had offered. > **""Give him his right.""**"
"6644","Musa ibn Umayr, from his father, reported that al-Husayn (رضي الله عنه) commanded a crier who called that no man upon whom is debt should come with them. A man said his wife would guarantee his debt, so Husayn asked what guarantee of a woman amounts to."
"6645","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded expulsion of Banu al-Nadir from Madinah, people from them came saying they had debts not yet due. He commanded reduction for prompt payment. > **""Remit and take promptly.""**"
"6646","The hadith of Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) has preceded that the Prophet ﷺ forbade certain matters, among them forbidding sale of deferred for prompt, meaning one has a thousand dirhams upon a man, so a man says: I will hasten five hundred for you and leave the remainder."
"6647","Abu al-Mu'arik reported that a man of Ghafiq had a hundred dinars upon a man of Mahrah in the time of Uthman (رضي الله عنه), the deferred hundred not yet due, and they gained a good booty. The Mahri said: I will hasten seventy dinars for you on condition you erase the hundred from me. The Ghafiqi agreed. Al-Miqdad (رضي الله عنه) passed by them, so the creditor seized his mount's bridle to make him witness. When the account was related to him he declared both had taken notice of war from Allah and His Messenger."
"6648","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Delay of the rich is injustice; when one of you is referred to a solvent one, let him follow.""**"
"6649","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade two sales in one sale, and said: > **""Delay of the rich is injustice, and when one of you is transferred to a solvent one, let him transfer.""**"
"6650","Ali (رضي الله عنه) reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, in another wording that Allah hates them: > **""Allah does not love the unjust rich, nor the ignorant shaykh, nor the arrogant poor. Indeed Allah detests the unjust rich, the ignorant shaykh, and the destitute arrogant.""**"
"6651","Khawlah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, declaring that Allah does not sanctify a nation where the weak cannot take his right from the strong without stammering, then explaining the state of the debtor whose creditor leaves satisfied, for whom beasts of earth and fish of water pray, and whose creditor leaves angry, against whom injustice is written every day, night, Friday and month. > **""Allah has not sanctified a nation where its weak cannot take his right from its strong without stammering. Whoever's creditor turns from his right satisfied with him, the beasts of the earth and the fish of the water pray for him; and whoever's creditor turns away angry, injustice is written against him every day, night, Friday and month.""**"
"6652","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever marries a woman upon a dower intending not to pay it to her is an adulterer; and whoever takes a debt intending not to pay it to its owner is a thief.""**"
"6653","Amr ibn Dinar, agent of al-Zubayr ibn Shu'ayb al-Basri, reported that the sons of Suhayb said to Suhayb (رضي الله عنه): O father, the sons of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrate from their fathers. He said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire. And I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire. Any man who marries a woman intending not to give her anything of her dower dies the day he dies as an adulterer. And any man who buys from a man a purchase intending not to give him anything of its price dies the day he dies as a traitor, and the traitor is in the Fire.""**"
"6654","Maymun al-Kurdi, from his father, reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Any man who marries a woman for little or much dower, with no intention in himself to pay her right, deceiving her, then dies without paying her right, will meet Allah on the Day of Resurrection as an adulterer. And any man who takes a debt with no intention to pay its owner his right, taking his wealth, then dies without paying his debt, will meet Allah as a thief.""**"
"6655","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever takes a debt intending to repay it, Allah will repay for him on the Day of Resurrection in truth. And whoever takes a debt with no intention to repay it, then dies, Allah Almighty will say on the Day of Resurrection: Did you think I would not take for My servant his right? Then his good deeds will be taken and placed in the good deeds of the other; if he has no good deeds, the sins of the other will be taken and placed upon him.""**"
"6656","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Debts are two: whoever dies intending its settlement, I am his guardian; and whoever dies with no intention of its settlement, he is one whose good deeds will be taken, when there is neither dinar nor dirham.""**"
"6657","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever of my Ummah bears a debt, then strives to settle it, then dies before settling it, I am his guardian.""**"
"6658","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) used to take debts, so it was said to her: What is with you and debt when you have no need of it? She said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""There is no servant with intention to repay his debt except he has help from Allah. So I seek that help.""**"
"6659","In another narration from Aishah (رضي الله عنها) from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning intention to repay debt: > **""Except he has from Allah help and protection.""**"
"6660","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported concerning one burdened with debt who is anxious for its settlement and strives to repay it, and her own love for that accompanying guard, with a full account mentioned in the narration. > **""Whoever has debt upon him and is concerned with repaying it, or strives to repay it, there remains with him a guard from Allah.""**"
"6661","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported in another wording concerning the one who intends repayment of debt. > **""For him is aid from Allah, and Allah will bring about provision for him.""**"
"6662","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the debtor brought on the Day of Resurrection and halted before his Lord, questioned about taking debt and neglecting the rights of people, excusing himself that he did not eat it nor drink it nor wear it nor waste it but was struck by burning, theft, or loss, whereupon Allah declares him truthful and most worthy to settle for him, placing something in the scale of his balance so his good deeds outweigh his evil deeds, entering Paradise by the bounty of His mercy. > **""Allah will call the debtor on the Day of Resurrection until he is halted before Him, and it will be said: O son of Adam, for what did you take this debt, and for what did you neglect the rights of people? He will say: My Lord, You know that I took it and did not eat, nor drink, nor wear, nor waste, but there came upon me either burning, or theft, or loss. Allah will say: My servant has spoken truthfully; I am most worthy to settle for you today. Then Allah will call for something and place it in the pan of his scale, so his good deeds outweigh his evil deeds, and he will enter Paradise by the bounty of His mercy.""**"
"6663","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning three situations in which one contracts debt then dies without repaying, for which Allah repays on his behalf: one in the path of Allah whose garment wears out fearing exposure of his nakedness, one who finds a dead Muslim with nothing to shroud him and cover him, and one who fears hardship upon himself and seeks chastity through marriage to a woman. > **""There are three: whoever contracts debt in them, then dies without repaying, Allah will repay for him: a man in the path of Allah whose garment wears out and he fears his nakedness will appear, then dies without repaying; a man with whom a Muslim dies and he finds nothing to shroud him nor to cover him, then dies without repaying; and a man who fears hardship upon himself and seeks chastity through marriage to a woman, then dies without repaying. Allah, Blessed and Exalted, will repay for him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6664","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning one who desires his supplication answered and his distress removed through granting relief to one in hardship, with the wording of easing the insolvent in another narration. > **""Whoever desires that his supplication be answered and his distress removed, let him grant relief to one in hardship.""**"
"6665","Uthman (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicate for shade for the servant who grants respite to one in hardship or forgoes the debt of one indebted. > **""May Allah shade a servant in His shade on a day when there is no shade but His shade: one who grants respite to one in hardship, or forgoes for one indebted.""**"
"6666","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out toward the mosque gesturing with his hand toward the ground concerning granting respite or remitting for one in hardship. > **""Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, or remits for him, Allah will protect him from the blaze of Hell.""**"
"6667","Kab ibn Ujrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning granting respite or easing for one in hardship. > **""Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, or eases upon him, Allah will shade him in His shade on a day when there is no shade but His shade.""**"
"6668","Asad ibn Zurarah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described what gladdens one seeking shade on the day without shade through easing or remitting for one in hardship. > **""Whoever is gladdened that Allah shade him on a day when there is no shade but His shade, let him ease upon one in hardship, or remit from him.""**"
"6669","Abu al-Darda (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning granting respite or remitting for one in hardship. > **""Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, or remits from him, Allah will shade him in His shade on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6670","Abu al-Yasar (رضي الله عنه) testified that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the first of people to seek shade in the shade of Allah on the Day of Resurrection as one who grants respite until ease or gives as charity what he seeks, saying it is charity seeking the Face of Allah, then tearing his document. > **""The first of people to seek shade in the shade of Allah on the Day of Resurrection is a man who grants respite to one in hardship until he finds something, or gives as charity to him what he seeks from him, saying: What is mine upon you is charity seeking the Face of Allah, and he tears his document.""**"
"6671","Shaddad ibn Aws (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise shade for granting respite or giving as charity to one in hardship. > **""Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, or gives as charity to him, Allah will shade him in His shade on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6672","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) testified by his beloved ﷺ that he heard him promise shade for granting respite to one in hardship or aiding one lacking skill. > **""Allah will shade in His shade on the Day of Resurrection whoever grants respite to one in hardship, or aids one lacking skill.""**"
"6673","Abu Qatadah (رضي الله عنه) and Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ described attaining salvation and shade beneath His Throne through granting respite to one in hardship. > **""Whoever is gladdened that Allah save him from the distresses of the Day of Resurrection, and shade him beneath His Throne, let him grant respite to one in hardship.""**"
"6674","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised shade for granting respite and described every good as charity. > **""Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, Allah will shade him in His shade on the Day of Resurrection, and every good is charity.""**"
"6675","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described recompense for one who grants respite to one in hardship until ease. > **""Whoever grants respite to one in hardship until his ease, Allah will grant him respite for his sin until his turn.""**"
"6676","Buraydah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ first mention charity like the debt for each day for granting respite, then mention charity like it doubled for each day, and upon questioning explained the distinction between before maturity and after maturity with continued respite. > **""Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him for each day is charity like it. Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him for each day is charity like it doubled. For him for each day is charity before the debt falls due; then when it falls due and he grants him respite, for him for each day is charity like it doubled.""**"
"6677","Imran ibn Husayn (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described charity for deferring a due right to its term and for deferring beyond its term day by day. > **""When a man has a right upon a man and he defers it to its term, for him is charity; then if he defers it after its term, for him for each day is charity.""**"
"6678","Jarir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ addressed the owner of a right concerning taking his due with dignity. > **""Take your right with dignity, complete or deficient.""**"
"6679","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported in a long narration that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out from Madinah toward the polytheists to fight them, his father charged him to remain among those watching in Madinah and expressed his expectation of martyrdom while leaving daughters behind, that his aunt brought his father and maternal uncle slain upon a camel to bury them in Madinah until a caller announced the command of the Prophet ﷺ to return the slain to be buried where they fell, that in the caliphate of Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan (رضي الله عنه) his father's grave was disturbed by workmen and he found him unchanged except what killing leaves, that his father left debt in dates and some creditors pressed him harshly, so he asked the Prophet ﷺ to assist him that the creditor grant respite from his palms until the coming harvest, the Prophet ﷺ promising to come near midday with his companions, entering with permission, and blessing his affair until Allah sufficed him. > **""Yes, I will come to you, if Allah wills, near midday.""**"
"6680","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that before the fighting of Uhud his father called him and told him he saw himself as the first slain among the companions of Muhammad ﷺ, leaving none dearer to him after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than Jabir, with debt upon him and sisters, enjoining good care of them and settlement of his debt, that he was indeed among the first slain and was buried with another in a grave, that after six months Jabir exhumed him finding him as on the day of burial except near his ear, that upon return to Madinah complaint was made that the creditor of Abdullah pressed Jabir, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came walking before Abu Bakr and Umar (رضي الله عنهما) asking the man to take part and defer part until the dates of the coming year, the man refusing, Umar (رضي الله عنه) speaking harshly to him, the Prophet ﷺ restraining Umar for the right-holder has speech, then touring the palms and commanding full payment with notice at harvest, sharing food with them at midday while Abu Bakr and Umar kept people back, and his wife supplicating for the Prophet ﷺ. > **""Take part, and defer part until the dates of the coming year. Gently, O Umar, for the owner of the right has speech. Give the one to whom is due fully and completely, and when you harvest inform me.""**"
"6681","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported in another wording that a man had ajwah dates due upon him with insufficient yield in his palms, he asked for deferment or settlement by account and was refused, then the Prophet ﷺ and Umar came and interceded, Umar nearly seizing the man, the Prophet ﷺ restraining him for it is his right, then entering the palms and looking at their tops and commanding notice at harvest, after which Jabir harvested, settled his dates in full, and ten or fifteen wasqs remained for him. > **""O so-and-so, take from Jabir and defer from him. O Umar, gently; it is his right. Go with us to your palms. O Jabir, when you harvest your palms inform me.""**"
"6682","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for guarantee of six traits for guarantee of Paradise, then mentioned not wronging in division of inheritances, doing justice to people from oneself, not showing cowardice when fighting the enemy, not misappropriating booty, and restraining one's oppressor from one's oppressed, while the sixth did not appear in the narration. > **""Guarantee six traits and I guarantee Paradise for you. Do not wrong in division of your inheritances. Do justice to people from yourselves. Do not show cowardice when fighting your enemy. Do not misappropriate your booty. Restrain your oppressor from your oppressed.""**"
"6683","Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about borrowing yeast-dough and bread and declared it from noble morals, permitting taking small and giving large and taking large and giving small, with the best being best in repayment. > **""Glory be to Allah! It is only from noble morals. Take small and give large, and take large and give small, and the best of you are best in repayment.""**"
"6684","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described one who walks to his creditor with his right, for whom the beasts of the earth and the fish of water invoke blessings, with a tree planted in Paradise for each step and a sin forgiven. > **""Whoever walks to his creditor with his right, the beasts of the earth and the fish of water invoke blessings upon him, and there is planted for him for each step a tree in Paradise, and a sin is forgiven.""**"
"6685","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the best through excellence in settling debts. > **""The best of you are best in repayment.""**"
"6686","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ purchased a camel, or camels, from a Bedouin for a wasq of stored dates, meaning ajwah, then returned to his house seeking the dates and not finding them, went out excusing himself that they had supposed it was with them, the Bedouin twice or thrice crying breach of trust while people rebuked him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ restraining them for the owner of the right has speech, then sending to Khawlah bint Hakim ibn Umayyah to lend a wasq of stored dates until Allah enables repayment, she consenting and sending one to take possession, whereupon he settled the man in full and supplicated for Khawlah. > **""Leave him, for the owner of the right has speech. O servant of Allah, we purchased from you a camel, and we supposed we had what we named for you, but we sought it and did not find it. Go to Khawlah bint Hakim ibn Umayyah and say to her: if you have a wasq of stored dates then lend to us until we repay you, if Allah wills. Go with him and settle him in full.""**"
"6687","Khawlah bint Qays (رضي الله عنها), wife of Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib (رضي الله عنه), reported that a wasq of dates was due from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to a man of Banu Saidah who came demanding it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding an Ansari man to settle him who settled him with dates inferior to his, the man refusing and declaring none worthier of justice than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the eyes of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ filling with tears in assent and warning that Allah does not sanctify a nation whose weak does not take his right from its strong without hardship, then commanding Khawlah to feed him and oil him and settle him, for no creditor leaves his debtor pleased but the beasts of the earth and the fish of the seas invoke blessings upon the debtor, and no servant who evades his creditor while able but Allah records against him sin each day and night. > **""He has spoken truthfully; who is worthier of justice than me?! Allah does not sanctify a nation whose weak does not take his right from its strong without hardship. O Khawlah, feed him and oil him, and settle him, for there is no creditor who leaves his debtor pleased but the beasts of the earth and the fish of the seas invoke blessings upon him, and there is no servant who evades his creditor while able but Allah records against him in each day and night a sin.""**"
"6688","Abdullah ibn Abi Sufyan (رضي الله عنه) reported that a Jew came demanding dates from the Prophet ﷺ and spoke harshly, his Companions rose against him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ restraining them that Allah does not sanctify, or have mercy upon, a nation whose weak do not take their right without hardship, then sending to Khawlah bint Hakim seeking a loan of dates and settling him, explaining that thus do the faithful fulfilling servants act, though dates had been with them but dusty. > **""Allah does not sanctify a nation whose weak do not take their right among them without hardship. Thus do the faithful fulfilling servants of Allah act. There were dates with us, but they were dusty.""**"
"6689","Abu Humayd al-Saidi (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ borrowed dates of a fine kind from a man who came demanding, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking delay until something comes, the man crying breach of trust, Umar speaking harshly, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ restraining him for the owner of the right has speech and sending to Khawlah bint Hakim al-Ansariyyah, with whom was found only stored dates, commanding settlement therewith, the man upon settlement testifying to full and good repayment, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ praised those who make repayment good. > **""Leave him, O Umar, for the owner of the right has speech. Go to Khawlah bint Hakim al-Ansariyyah and seek dates with her. Take and settle. Have you received in full? The best servants of Allah of this nation are those who make repayment good.""**"
"6690","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ borrowed forty sas from a man of the Ansar who became needy and came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informing him nothing had yet come, the man wishing to speak, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding only good speech for he is best of borrowers, then giving him forty in excess and forty for his loan, eighty in all. > **""Nothing has yet come to us. Speak only good, for I am the best of those who borrow. Take forty in excess and forty for your loan.""**"
"6691","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ demanding half a wasq he had lent, and he gave him a wasq, half for his due and half from himself, then the owner of a wasq came demanding and he gave him two wasqs, a wasq for his due and a wasq from himself. > **""Half a wasq is yours and half a wasq for you from me. A wasq is yours and a wasq for you from me.""**"
"6692","Ata ibn Yaqub reported that Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) borrowed a thousand dirhams from him then repaid him with better than it, and upon remarking that his dirhams were better than his own, explained the excess as a gift from himself."
"6693","Al-Talib reported that he was with the Prophet ﷺ who fed him and measured out for him a mudd which he lifted and ate with people until it became food, then Al-Talib came to the Prophet ﷺ mentioning he had gathered what was fed to him from such-and-such day until today, and the Prophet ﷺ borrowed it from him and measured out for him from it what he used to measure before that."
"6694","Al-Qasim ibn Abdullah reported that maternal uncles of his of Banu Asad borrowed, then when their stipends came forth choice was made for them from their wealth, and when it was brought Abdullah said it is better than the wealth given to them and commanded gathering their stipends and giving from their goods."
"6695","Samurah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say concerning one who mortgages land for debt upon him, that he settles from its fruits what exceeds after its upkeep, reckoned from the time of liability after accounting justly for the labour and upkeep of the one holding it. > **""Whoever mortgages land for debt upon him, he settles from its fruits what exceeds after its upkeep, and that is settled for him from that time of his liability after there is reckoned for its holder who has it his labour and his upkeep with justice.""**"
"6696","Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani reported that while in Egypt a man directed him to a man of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ, who identified himself as Saraq, explaining the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him so and he would not abandon it, for he had purchased two camels from a desert man promising payment, then entered his house and left from behind and went away, selling them and fulfilling his need, hiding until supposing the Bedouin had left, then being seized and brought before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who questioned his motive and commanded payment, and upon his claim of nothing, named him Saraq and handed him to the Bedouin to sell until recovering his right, people bargaining for him while he asked their intent to buy or ransom, then declaring none of them needier than himself and freeing him. > **""What carried you to what you did? Settle him. You are Saraq. Go with him, O Bedouin, and sell him until you recover your right.""**"
"6697","Abd al-Rahman al-Qayni reported that Saraq purchased wheat brought by a man who had recited al-Baqarah, then evaded him, then the man seized him and brought him to the Prophet ﷺ who commanded his sale, so he went forth with him while the companions of the Prophet ﷺ bargained for three days, then it seemed fit to the creditor and he freed him. > **""Sell Saraq.""**"
"6698","Kab ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ restricted the wealth of Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) and sold it for debt that was upon him."
"6699","Kab ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه), one of the three to whom Allah turned in mercy, reported that Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) contracted debt in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until it encompassed his wealth, though of the righteous companions, explaining to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that since embracing Islam he had resolved not to withhold owned wealth and had spent his wealth in the cause of Islam leaving great debt, asking him to call his creditors and seek gentleness, and if refused to hand over his wealth, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called them and proposed gentleness but they declared love of their wealth, whereupon he handed over all the wealth of Muadh to them, then sent Muadh to part of Yemen to mend his state, where he obtained wealth through the offices of leadership, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passing away while Muadh was in Yemen, Muadh fighting apostates of Yemen with the commanders appointed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until they entered Islam, then coming in the caliphate of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (رضي الله عنه) with great wealth, Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) advising him to come to Abu Bakr and seek it lawful, Muadh replying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him only to mend him after surrender of his wealth and he would hand nothing unless asked, then Abu Bakr questioning and releasing it to him."
"6700","The son of Kab ibn Malik reported that Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) was a handsome generous youth of the best youth of his people, granting whatever was asked until he contracted debt closing his wealth, he spoke to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to speak to his creditors and he did but they remitted nothing, and if any were left through intercession it would have been Muadh through the speech of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called him and remained until selling his wealth and dividing it among his creditors, Muadh standing with no wealth, then after Hajj the Prophet ﷺ sent him to Yemen to mend his state, he being the first to voyage in that wealth, coming to Abu Bakr from Yemen after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had passed away."
"6701","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning the bankrupt with whom a man finds his property intact by itself that he is most entitled to it. > **""When a man becomes bankrupt and a man finds his property, meaning with the bankrupt, intact by itself, he is most entitled to it.""**"
"6702","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning one bankrupt with whom a man finds his property while having taken nothing from his wealth that he is most entitled to it. > **""Whichever man becomes bankrupt and a man finds with him his property, while he had taken nothing from his wealth, he is most entitled to it.""**"
"6703","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded rendering the trust to its entruster and forbade betraying one who betrayed. > **""Render the trust to one who entrusted you, and betray not one who betrayed you.""**"
"6704","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ command rendering the trust and forbidding betrayal of the betrayer. > **""Render the trust to one who entrusted you, and betray not one who betrayed you.""**"
"6705","Abu Qirad al-Sulami (رضي الله عنه) reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he called for water for purification, dipped his hand therein and performed ablution, and they followed it and drank it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ questioning their motive, they replying love of Allah and His Messenger, whereupon he directed them to rendering when entrusted, truthfulness when speaking, and excellence to the neighbour for the love of Allah and His Messenger. > **""What carried you to what you did? If you love that Allah and His Messenger love you, then render when you are entrusted, speak truthfully when you speak, and do good to the neighbour who neighbours you.""**"
"6706","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned four traits in one leaving no harm from what is missed of the world: guarding trust, good character, truthful speech, and chastity in food. > **""There are four: when they are in you, no harm upon you for what you miss of the world: guarding trust, good character, truthful speech, and chastity in food.""**"
"6707","Shaddad ibn Aws (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that the first of worldly religion to be lost is the trust. > **""The first of your worldly religion you will lose is the trust.""**"
"6708","Anas (رضي الله عنه) enjoined fear of Allah and rendering trusts to their people."
"6709","Ubadah ibn al-Samit (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ called for guarantee of six from oneself for guarantee of Paradise: truthfulness in speech, fulfilment when promising, rendering when entrusted, guarding private parts, lowering gazes, and restraining hands. > **""Guarantee for me six from yourselves and I guarantee Paradise for you: speak truthfully when you speak, fulfil when you promise, render when you are entrusted, guard your private parts, lower your gazes, and restrain your hands.""**"
"6710","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the borrowed item due for return. > **""The borrowed item is to be returned.""**"
"6711","Said reported from one who heard the Prophet ﷺ declare the rulings of borrowing, granting, debt, and surety. > **""Indeed the borrowed is to be returned, and the granted is to be returned, and the debt is to be settled, and the guarantor is liable.""**"
"6712","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade returning the gift and striking the Muslims. > **""Do not return the gift, and do not strike the Muslims.""**"
"6713","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded giving to one who asks by Allah, granting refuge to one who seeks refuge by Allah, answering one who invites, and accepting as gift even a shank, with the wording in another narration of a forearm or a shank. > **""Whoever asks you by Allah, give him; and whoever seeks refuge with you by Allah, grant him refuge; and whoever invites you, answer him; and whoever gifts to you a shank, accept it. Whoever gifts to you a forearm or a shank, accept it.""**"
"6714","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Muslims used to exchange gifts in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as a bond between them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said if people embraced Islam they would exchange gifts without poverty, with the wording in the larger collection that the Prophet ﷺ commanded the gift as a bond between people and said if people embraced Islam they would exchange gifts without hunger. > **""If people embrace Islam, they will exchange gifts without poverty. If people embrace Islam, they will exchange gifts without hunger.""**"
"6715","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the Ansar commanding mutual gifting for it removes ill-feeling and bequeaths love, swearing he would accept even a trotter as gift and answer an invitation even to a forearm. > **""O company of Ansar, exchange gifts, for the gift removes ill-feeling and bequeaths love. By Allah, if a trotter were gifted to me I would accept, and if I were invited to a forearm I would answer.""**"
"6716","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded mutual gifting for love, migration for bequeathing glory to children, and overlooking the slips of the noble. > **""Exchange gifts, you will love one another; and migrate, you will bequeath your children glory; and overlook the slips of the noble.""**"
"6717","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the believing women commanding mutual gifting even with a sheep's trotter for it confirms love and removes hatreds. > **""O believing women, exchange gifts even with a sheep's trotter, for it confirms love and removes hatreds.""**"
"6718","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded mutual gifting for increase in love. > **""Exchange gifts, you will increase in love.""**"
"6719","Umm Hakim bint Wadda al-Khuzaiyyah (رضي الله عنها) reported that she heard the Prophet ﷺ command mutual gifting for it multiplies love and removes the evils of the breast. > **""Exchange gifts, for the gift multiplies love and removes the evils of the breast.""**"
"6720","Al-Husayn ibn Ali (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Prophet ﷺ praising the gift before need. > **""Excellent is the thing, the gift before need.""**"
"6721","Abdullah ibn Busr (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to accept the gift and not accept charity."
"6722","Umm Salamah (رضي الله عنها) reported concerning a woman who gifted to her a sheep's leg that had been given to the giver as charity, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ commanded her to accept it."
"6723","Abdullah ibn Busr (رضي الله عنه), companion of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that his mother used to send him with gifts to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he accepted them from him."
"6724","Abdullah ibn Busr (رضي الله عنه) reported in another wording that his sister used to send him with something to the Prophet ﷺ as a present and he accepted it from him."
"6725","Abdullah ibn Busr (رضي الله عنه) reported that his mother sent him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a cluster of grapes which he ate, his mother asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether Abdullah brought a cluster, he answering no, thereafter whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him calling him treacherous. > **""No. Treacherous, treacherous.""**"
"6726","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted at a dwelling and a woman sent with her son a ewe, he milked it and commanded carrying it to her mother, and she drank until quenched, then another ewe was brought and he milked it commanding drink for Abu Bakr, then another ewe was brought and he milked it then drank himself. > **""Carry it to your mother. Give drink to Abu Bakr.""**"
"6727","Umm Kulthum bint Abi Salamah (رضي الله عنها) reported that when the Prophet ﷺ married Umm Salamah (رضي الله عنها) he told her he had gifted to the Negus a suit and measures of musk, deeming the Negus dead and his gift returned to him, promising it to her if returned, and it was as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, his gift returned to him, so he gave each of his wives a measure of musk and gave Umm Salamah the remainder of the musk and the suit. > **""I gifted to the Negus a suit and measures of musk, and I deem the Negus dead, and I deem my gift returned to me; if it is returned to me it is yours.""**"
"6728","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared those present as partners in a gift gifted to one in their presence. > **""Whoever is gifted a gift while people are with him, they are partners with him therein.""**"
"6729","Al-Hasan ibn Ali (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared those sitting with one to whom a gift comes as partners therein. > **""Whoever receives a gift while people sit with him, they are partners with him therein.""**"
"6730","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a Bedouin gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a gift, in one wording a she-camel, and he recompensed him therefor asking his pleasure, the man twice refusing, so he increased until the man was pleased, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared his resolve to accept gifts only from Quraysh, Ansar, or Thaqif. > **""Are you pleased? I had resolved not to accept a gift except from Qurayshi, or Ansari, or Thaqafi.""**"
"6731","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported concerning a man nicknamed Donkey who used to gift to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a skin of clarified butter and a skin of honey, then when its owner came demanding, he brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying give this one the price of his goods, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did no more than smile and command that he be given."
"6732","Umm Sunbulah (رضي الله عنها) reported that she came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a gift which the wives of the Prophet ﷺ refused to take saying they take no gift, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came commanding them to take the gift of Umm Sunbulah for she is the desert-dweller and they are her townsfolk, and he granted her such-and-such valley which Abdullah ibn Hasan ibn Hasan ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib later purchased from them, and gave her young camels, part of which Amr ibn Qayzi saw. > **""Take the gift of Umm Sunbulah, for she is our desert-dweller and we are her townsfolk.""**"
"6733","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that Umm Sunbulah gifted milk to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and did not find him, so she told her the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden them to eat the food of the Bedouins, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered with Abu Bakr (رضي الله عنه) asking what she had, she replying milk gifted to him, commanding her to pour and pass to Abu Bakr, then to pour and pass to Aishah who drank, then to pour and pass to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who drank the most wholesome and cooling milk for the liver, Aishah questioning the prohibition, whereupon he explained they are not Bedouins but people of the desert while they are people of the town, responding when called, so not Bedouins. > **""Pour, O Umm Sunbulah. Pass to Abu Bakr. Pour, O Umm Sunbulah. Pass to Aishah. Pour, O Umm Sunbulah. O Aishah, they are not Bedouins; they are our desert-dwellers and we are their townsfolk, and when they are called they answer, so they are not Bedouins.""**"
"6734","Iyad ibn Abdullah reported from his father that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to whom a man gifted a skin of honey and he accepted it, the man asking for protection of his mountain pass, so he protected it for him and wrote a document for him."
"6735","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning recompense for a gift. > **""Whoever gives as a gift even a trotter, then recompense him.""**"
"6736","Umm Hakim bint Wadda al-Khuzaiyyah (رضي الله عنها) said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the recompense of the rich from the poor? He said: Sincerity and supplication. I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you dislike returning kindness? He said: > **""How ugly it is. Had a trotter been gifted to me I would accept it, and had I been invited to a foreleg I would answer.""**"
"6737","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever receives a favour and mentions it has thanked for it, and whoever adorns himself with what he has not attained is like one wearing two garments of falsehood.""**"
"6738","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""When a man says to his brother: May Allah reward you with good, he has excelled in praise.""**"
"6739","Anas (رضي الله عنه) said: When Musa (عليه السلام), the Prophet of Allah, called his companion to the term that was between them, his companion said to him: Every sheep that bears young unlike its colour, its young is yours. So he set out and placed ropes upon the water, and when they saw the ropes they were startled and wheeled about, and all of them bore speckled young except one sheep, so he took their young that year."
"6740","Utbah ibn al-Nuddar (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked: Which of the two terms did Musa fulfil? He said: The most righteous of them and the most complete of them. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: When Musa (عليه السلام) intended to part from Shuayb (عليهما السلام), he commanded his wife to ask her father to give her from his sheep what they might live by, so he gave her what his sheep bore that year of young of altered colour. He said: No sheep passed but Musa struck its sides with his staff, so all of them bore young of their altered colours, and they bore two and thirty, no sheep among them fashush, nor dabub, nor kamishah missing the palm, nor thaul. And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you conquer Sham you will find remnants of them, and they are the Samiriyyah.""**"
"6741","Abdullah ibn Sakhr ibn Lawdhan (رضي الله عنه), who was among those the Prophet ﷺ sent with the officials to Yemen, said: The Prophet ﷺ said to Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) when he sent him as a teacher to Yemen: So when he returned he returned with thirty heads that had been gifted to him. > **""I have known your trial in religion, and I have made the gift lawful for you; if anything is gifted to you, then accept it.""**"
"6742","Abu Humayd al-Saidi (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The gifts of rulers are misappropriation.""**"
"6743","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The gifts of rulers are misappropriation.""**"
"6744","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""The gift to the Imam is misappropriation.""**"
"6745","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""The gifts of rulers are misappropriation.""**"
"6746","Ismah (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The gift takes away hearing and sight.""**"
"6747","Irak ibn Malik reported that Hakim ibn Hizam (رضي الله عنه) said: Muhammad ﷺ was the most beloved of men to me among people in Jahiliyyah. When he proclaimed prophethood and went out to Madinah, Hakim ibn Hizam witnessed the season while he was a disbeliever, and found a hullah of Dhi Yazan being sold, so he bought it for fifty dinars to gift it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He came with it to him in Madinah and wished him to accept it as a gift, but he refused. Ubaydullah said: I think he said: So when he refused my gift I gave it to him for a price. Then he wore it, and I saw it upon him on the minbar, and I never saw anything more beautiful than him in it that day. Then he gave it to Usamah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنهما), and Hakim saw it upon Usamah and said: O Usamah, do you wear the hullah of Dhi Yazan? He said: Yes, by Allah, for I am better than Dhi Yazan, and my father is better than his father. Hakim said: So I went to the people of Makkah marvelling at the saying of Usamah. > **""We do not accept anything from the polytheists, but if you wish we will take it for a price.""**"
"6748","Imran ibn Husayn (رضي الله عنه) reported that Iyad ibn Himar al-Mujashii, then al-Nahshali (رضي الله عنه), gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a horse before he accepted Islam, whereupon he said: > **""I dislike the gifts of the polytheists.""**"
"6749","Amir ibn Malik, who is called Mulaib al-Asinnah, said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a gift, whereupon he said: > **""We do not accept the gift of a polytheist.""**"
"6750","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr (رضي الله عنهما) said: Qutaylah bint Abd al-Uzza ibn Asad ibn Malik ibn Hisl came to her daughter Asma bint Abi Bakr (رضي الله عنها) with gifts of dabb lizards, lupine and ghee, but Asma refused to accept her gift and to admit her into her house. So Aishah (رضي الله عنها) asked the Prophet ﷺ, and Allah the Exalted revealed: Allah does not forbid you concerning those who did not fight you in religion, to the end of the verse. So he commanded her to accept her gift and to admit her into her house. > **""Allah does not forbid you concerning those who did not fight you in religion.""**"
"6751","Buraydah (رضي الله عنه) said: Al-Muqawqis the Copt gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two slave-girls, one of them Mariyah (رضي الله عنها), the mother of Ibrahim son of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the other the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bestowed upon Hassan ibn Thabit (رضي الله عنه), and she is the mother of Abd al-Rahman ibn Hassan. And he gifted to him a mule, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted that."
"6752","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that the king of Dhi Yazan gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a jar of manna, and he accepted it."
"6753","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) said: Al-Muqawqis, the ruler of Alexandria, gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a kohl stick of Shami idan wood, a mirror and a comb."
"6754","Hanzalah ibn al-Rabi al-Katib (رضي الله عنه) said: Al-Muqawqis, king of the Copts, gifted to the Prophet ﷺ a gift and a grey mule, and he ﷺ accepted it."
"6755","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) said: Al-Muqawqis gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a cup of glass, then he mentioned the hadith."
"6756","Anas (رضي الله عنه) said: Al-Ukaydir gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a jar of manna. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned from prayer he passed by the people, giving every man among them a piece, and he gave Jabir (رضي الله عنه) a piece. Then he returned to him and gave him another piece, so he said: You already gave me once? He said: > **""This is for the daughters of Abdullah.""**"
"6757","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that al-Hajjaj ibn Ilat al-Sulami (رضي الله عنه) gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ his sword Dhu al-Faqar, and Dihyah al-Kalbi (رضي الله عنه) gifted to him a grey mule."
"6758","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""One who returns in his gift is like one who returns in his vomit.""**"
"6759","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Equalize among your children in giving; had I been preferring anyone I would have preferred women.""**"
"6760","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Any man who grants his son a grant and the son separates it, then the father becomes needy, the son has more right to it; but if the son has not separated it, the father has more right to it.""**"
"6761","Yala ibn Murrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to a man: He said: It is yours, O Messenger of Allah. So he branded it with the brand of charity, then sent it forth. > **""Gift me this camel or sell it to me.""**"
"6762","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to a man: > **""You and your wealth belong to your father.""**"
"6763","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) said: A man came seeking aid against his father, saying: He takes my wealth. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: And in one narration: The child is from the earning of the father. > **""You and your wealth are from your father's earning.""**"
"6764","Umar (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: My father wishes to take my wealth. He said: > **""You and your wealth belong to your father.""**"
"6765","Samurah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to a man: > **""You and your wealth belong to your father.""**"
"6766","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The child is from the earning of the father.""**"
"6767","Abu Burdah ibn Niyar (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best earning of a man is his child, and every righteous sale.""**"
"6768","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to a man: > **""You and your wealth belong to your father.""**"
"6769","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have wealth and dependents, and my father wishes to take from my wealth to his wealth? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You and your wealth belong to your father.""**"
"6770","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, my father has taken my wealth. So the Prophet ﷺ said: Go and bring me your father. Then Jibril (عليه السلام) descended upon the Prophet ﷺ and said: Allah the Exalted conveys Salam to you and says to you: When the old man comes to you, ask him about something he said within himself which his ears have not heard. When the old man came, the Prophet ﷺ said to him: What is it with your son complaining of you, do you wish to take his wealth? He said: Ask him, O Messenger of Allah, whether I spent it except upon one of his aunts paternal or maternal, or upon myself? So the Prophet ﷺ said: Leave this aside; inform me about something you said within yourself which your ears have not heard. So the old man said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, Allah keeps increasing us in certainty through you; I did say something within myself which my ears have not heard. He said: Say, and I am listening. He said: I said: I nourished you as a newborn and sustained you as a youth, you drank from what I earn for you and you drink deeply. When a night brought you sickness I did not sleep for your sickness but wakeful, tossing about. As though I were the one struck instead of you with what you were struck with, apart from me, while my eye sheds tears. My soul fears death for you, though it knows that death is a time appointed. Then when you reached the age and the goal to which the extent of my hope in you aimed, you made my recompense harshness and sternness, as though you are the bestower, the bounteous. Would that, when you did not observe the right of fatherhood, you had done as the neighbouring neighbour does. You appear devoted to opposition as though charged with answering the people of right with refusal. The narrator said: Thereupon the Prophet ﷺ took the son by his garment and said: > **""You and your wealth belong to your father.""**"
"6771","Qays ibn Abi Hazim (رحمه الله) said: I was present with Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (رضي الله عنه) when a man came to him and said: O Khalifah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, this one wishes to take all my wealth and consume it. So Abu Bakr said to him: What do you say? He said: Yes. So Abu Bakr said: You have from his wealth only what suffices you. He said: O Khalifah of the Messenger of Allah, did not the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: So Abu Bakr said to him: Be content with what Allah the Exalted is content with. > **""You and your wealth belong to your father.""**"
"6772","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Any man who grants his son a grant and the son separates it, then the father becomes needy, the son has more right to it; but if the son has not separated it, the father has more right to it.""**"
"6773","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) freed a slave of his and said: Indeed your wealth is mine, but I have left it for you."
"6774","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man of the Ansar gave his mother a garden of palms for her lifetime, then she died, so his brothers came and said: We share in it equally. He refused, so they disputed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he divided it among them as inheritance."
"6775","Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Umra is permissible for its people.""**"
"6776","In a narration from him: Umra is like inheritance. > **""Umra is like inheritance.""**"
"6777","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man granted another umra, then asked the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon he said, or as he said: > **""It belongs to his heirs.""**"
"6778","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Any man who is granted umra, it is for him and for his posterity after him, intending thereby whoever inherits him of his posterity; and whoever is granted ruqba, it is like umra.""**"
"6779","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Do not give ruqba, or do not give umra, for if you do then it is umra and marqab. The narrator said: I said: How is that? He said: Umra is that you say: It is yours for your lifetime. And ruqba is that you say: It is for the survivor of me and you. > **""Do not give ruqba, and do not give umra, for if you do then it is umra and marqab.""**"
"6780","Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever the polytheists grant land, there is no land for him.""**"
"6781","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Whoever revives rugged land of the town or dead land of the town, it is his.""**"
"6782","Umm Salamah (رضي الله عنها) heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""There is no man who revives land so that a thirsty liver drinks from it, or one seeking well-being takes from it, except Allah writes for him reward thereby.""**"
"6783","Fadalah ibn Ubayd (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The land is Allah's land, and the servants are Allah's servants; whoever revives dead land, it is his.""**"
"6784","Amr ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Whoever revives dead land that is not the right of a Muslim, it is his, and there is no right for an unjust root.""**"
"6785","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: And in a narration Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz (رحمه الله) said to Urwah (رحمه الله): Do you testify that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said this? He said: I testify that Aishah narrated this to me from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I testify that Aishah did not lie to me. > **""Whoever revives dead land, it is his, and there is no right for an unjust root.""**"
"6786","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever revives dead land, it is his, and there is no right for an unjust root.""**"
"6787","Umm Salamah (رضي الله عنها) reported that she used to delouse the head of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when Zaynab, wife of Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنها), came and began speaking to me while I spoke to her, and I raised my sight to her. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Turn to her with your ears, for you do not speak to her with your eyes. Zaynab said: I began complaining of the narrowness of the dwelling, whereupon he said: This is as the wife of Uthman ibn Mazun did; what descended upon her did not suffice her until it descended upon her head. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: So she inherited her share of the house of Abdullah and secured her house in Madinah. > **""Likewise, whoever of the migrant women marks a plot in Madinah, her plot is hers.""**"
"6788","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ reserved al-Baqi for horses. The narrator said: I said to him: For his horses? He said: No, except for the horses of the Muslims."
"6789","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Prophet ﷺ reserved al-Rabadhah for the camels of charity."
"6790","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no reservation except for Allah and for His Messenger.""**"
"6791","Sad ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The neighbour has more right to his saqab.""**"
"6792","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""The neighbour has more right to his saqab, whatever it be.""**"
"6793","Abu Rafi (رضي الله عنه) sold a portion that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had granted him, by the house of Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (رضي الله عنه), for eight thousand dirhams. A man had preceded offering ten thousand dirhams for it, but he refused to sell from him. Abu Rafi said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: And Sad was the nearest in proximity. > **""The people of the courtyard have more right to their courtyard.""**"
"6794","Yazid ibn al-Aswad (رضي الله عنه) said: I recited to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one hundred rhymes from the poetry of Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt; whenever I passed a verse he said: More. And I heard him say in that gathering: > **""The neighbour has more right to his saqab.""**"
"6795","Ubadah ibn al-Samit (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ decreed preemption among partners."
"6796","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Preemption is in all that boundaries have not fallen; when boundaries fall there is no preemption.""**"
"6797","Zayd ibn Thabit (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When boundaries fall there is no preemption.""**"
"6798","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The child retains his preemption until he attains maturity; when he attains maturity, if he wishes he takes, and if he wishes he leaves.""**"
"6799","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There is no preemption for a Christian.""**"
"6800","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The limit of the road is seven cubits.""**"
"6801","Ubadah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ decreed concerning a courtyard whose people wish to build therein that seven cubits be left between them for the road. And in a narration: He decreed concerning a courtyard among people that the road is seven cubits. > **""Seven cubits be left between them for the road.""**"
"6802","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Cursed is whoever turns to other than his patrons, cursed is whoever claims to other than his father, cursed is whoever changes the landmarks of the earth.""**"
"6803","Amr ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever changes the boundaries of the earth, upon him is the curse of Allah and His wrath on the Day of Resurrection; Allah accepts from him neither sarf nor adl.""**"
"6804","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Let none of you prevent his believing brother from placing wood upon his wall.""**"
"6805","In a narration from him: A man has the right to place his wood upon the wall of his neighbour. > **""A man has the right to place his wood upon the wall of his neighbour.""**"
"6806","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a wall, let him support upon the wall of his brother.""**"
"6807","Abu Shurayh al-Kabi (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What does the neighbour hope from his neighbour when he does not raise wood for him upon his wall?""**"
"6808","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""Whose neighbour asks him to fix wood in his wall, let him not prevent him.""**"
"6809","Amir ibn Rabiah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ decreed concerning the stream of Mahzur: > **""The upper holds back from the lower until it reaches the ankles, then he releases to the lower.""**"
"6810","Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) said concerning water that passes by gardens: the people below the stream are in authority over the people above it."
"6811","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (رضي الله عنه), when he handed over property as mudarabah, stipulated upon his partner that he would not travel with it by sea, nor encamp with it in a valley, nor purchase with it a living creature with a moist liver, and if he did so he would be liable. His stipulation was raised to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he permitted it."
"6812","Amr ibn Wathilah or Amir ibn Wathilah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Hakim ibn Hizam (رضي الله عنه) a dinar and commanded him to buy with it a sacrificial animal. He bought, then one came who offered him profit, so he sold, then he bought again, then he came to the Prophet ﷺ with a dinar and a sheep. He said: He said: O Messenger of Allah, I bought and sold and profited. So the Prophet ﷺ said to him: And he took the dinar and gave it in charity, and took the sheep and sacrificed it. > **""What is this?""** > **""May Allah bless for you in your trade.""**"
"6813","Salman (رضي الله عنه) said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ with food while I was a slave, and I said: This is charity. So he commanded his Companions and they ate, and he did not eat. Then I came to him with food and said: This is a gift I present to you honouring you, for I have seen that you do not eat charity. So he commanded his Companions and they ate, and he ate with them."
"6814","Salman (رضي الله عنه) said: I had sought permission of my mistress concerning that, and she made it lawful for me. So I gathered firewood and sold it, and I bought that food with it."
"6815","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a black slave came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: A wayfarer passes by me while I am among the livestock of my master; shall I give him to drink from its milk without his permission? He said: He said: I shoot and the game disappears from sight or does not disappear. He said: > **""No.""** > **""Eat what does not disappear from you, and leave what disappears.""**"
"6816","Abu Said al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is not lawful for one who believes in Allah and the Last Day to untie the bound udder of a she-camel without permission of its owners, for it is their seal upon it. When you are in a barren land and you see the milk-skin or waterskin or vessel of milk, then call the owners of the camels three times; if they give you to drink, then drink, and if not, then do not. If you are destitute and have no food with you, then let two men of you hold it, then drink.""**"
"6817","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey and our provisions ran out. We came upon camels whose udders were tied with bark of trees, and the people hastened to milk them. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: Then he said: > **""Perhaps there is in this the provision of a Muslim household. Would you like it if they came upon what is in your provision bags and took it?""** > **""If you must do so, then drink, but do not carry away.""**"
"6818","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is lawful for one of us from the property of his brother? He said: > **""Let him eat and not carry away, and let him drink and not carry away.""**"
"6819","Umayr, freedman of Abi al-Lahm (رضي الله عنه), said: I came with my masters intending emigration until, when we neared Madinah, they left me behind with their mounts. Severe hunger struck me. One who came out from Madinah passed by me and said: If you entered Madinah you would obtain dates from its gardens. So I entered a garden and cut two clusters. The owner of the garden came to me and brought me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of my affair, while upon me were two garments. He said: So I pointed to one of them. He said: And he let me go free. > **""Which of them is better?""** > **""Take it, and give the other to the owner of the garden.""**"
"6820","Umayr (رضي الله عنه) also said in a narration with Ahmad: I was herding at Dhat al-Jaysh and need befell me. I mentioned that to some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and they directed me to a garden of one of the Ansar. So I cut some clusters, and they seized me and took me to the Prophet ﷺ. I informed him of my need, so he gave me one cluster and returned the rest to its owners."
"6821","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command hospitality, and he forbade that the livestock of a man be milked except by his permission, saying: > **""Its milk is only like what is in your purses; neither is more lawful than the other.""**"
"6822","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Every pasturing and returning animal belonging to a people is forbidden to others.""**"
"6823","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that a Bedouin came asking him concerning what is forbidden to him and what is lawful for him, concerning his offspring and his livestock, concerning his goat and the fara from the newborn of his camels and sheep. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: And a man then asked him: What is my poverty, and what shall I eat thereof when I reach it, and what is my sufficiency that suffices me from it? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: The Bedouin said: What is my sufficiency that I leave it when I find it? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: And he commanded him with an atirah of sheep, one atirah from every hundred. > **""Lawful for you are the good things, and forbidden to you are the foul, except that you become in need of food not lawful to you, then eat thereof until you are sufficed by it.""** > **""When you expect newborn young, then sustain yourself with the meat of your livestock until your newborn, or you expect rain bringing herbage for you, then sustain yourself thereuntil from the meat of your livestock, or you expect provision reaching you, then sustain yourself from the meat of your livestock. If you expect nothing of that, then feed your family as seems fit to you until you are sufficed.""** > **""When you have quenched your family with an evening drink of milk, then avoid what is forbidden to you of food. As for your property, all of it is eased, with no unlawful therein, except that in your newborn camels is a fara and in your newborn sheep is a fara which your livestock nourishes until it is sufficed, then if you wish feed it to your family, and if you wish give its meat in charity.""**"
"6824","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It will not harm one of you what he repels hunger with, when he obtains it from the lawful.""**"
"6825","Mikhwal al-Nahdi, then al-Sulami, who had reached both Jahiliyyah and Islam (رضي الله عنه), said: I set my snares at al-Abwa, and a gazelle fell into one rope and carried off the rope. I went out in its tracks following it, and I found a man who had taken it. We disputed concerning it to the Prophet ﷺ, and we found him encamped at al-Abwa beneath a tree, shaded by leather. So he judged it between us in two halves. I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is my rope upon its leg. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we come to water and thirsty camels come upon us; we give them water from the water — is there reward for us in that? He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, the stray camels we meet, tied up, while we are hungry? He said: Then the Prophet ﷺ began to say: — or he said: from its hairs — The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it three times. I said: O Messenger of Allah, advise me. He said: > **""It is that.""** > **""Yes; for you in every living moist liver is reward.""** > **""Call: O owner of the camels. If he comes, well, otherwise untie its binding, milk and drink, then restore its binding, and leave for the milk its callers.""** > **""There will come upon people a time when the best property therein will be sheep between the two mosques — meaning the mosque of Madinah and the mosque of Makkah — eating trees and coming to waters. Its owner will eat from its fats and wear from its wools""** > **""while tribulations tremble among the hordes of the Arabs, and blood is shed.""** > **""Fear Allah, establish prayer, give zakah, perform Hajj and Umrah, be dutiful to your parents, join your kin, honour the guest, command the good, forbid the evil, and remain with the truth wherever it remains.""**"
"6826","Abu Said (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were struck by hunger in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Two men came until they overlooked gardens, and there were dates in a garden. One of them descended and the other held back. He ate until, when he was full, he began gathering into his clothes. The owner of the garden came, seized his garment, bound him to a palm, took a splinter and beat him painfully, then brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: I found this one in my garden eating until, when full, he gathered into his clothes. The other said: O Messenger of Allah, I and my companion came hungry; as for me I descended, and as for my companion he held back. I ate and took for my companion. So this one came and did such and such to me. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Go, give him his garment, and measure for him a wasq in place of what you struck him.""**"
"6827","Abu Waqid (رضي الله عنه) said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we are in a land where hunger strikes us; what is lawful for us of carrion? He said: > **""When you have not taken morning drink, nor evening drink, and have not gathered greens, then it is yours.""**"
"6828","Abu Waqid (رضي الله عنه) reported that a people lost a mule by death and had nothing to eat. They came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he permitted them concerning it."
"6829","Numan ibn Bashir (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever ties a beast upon the road of the Muslims is liable.""**"
"6830","Abu Ufayr Arif ibn Sari reported that a man asked Amr ibn al-As (رضي الله عنه), saying: A man in my care — I gave him a slave-girl in charity, then he died and I am his heir? Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) said to him: I shall inform you of what I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) carried upon a horse in the path of Allah, then he found its owner had put it up for sale and wanted to buy it, so he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He forbade him from it and said: > **""When you give charity, then let it proceed.""**"
"6831","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that al-Zubayr (رضي الله عنه) carried upon a horse in the path of Allah, and its owner wasted it. Al-Zubayr wanted to buy it, so the Prophet ﷺ forbade him from returning to his charity."
"6832","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, I gave my mother a garden in her lifetime, and she died leaving no heir but me. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has returned your garden to you and accepted your charity.""**"
"6833","Ali and Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: And in addition from Ali alone: > **""The promise is a debt.""** > **""Woe to one who promises then breaks, woe to one who promises then breaks, woe to one who promises then breaks.""**"
"6834","Qabath ibn Ashyam al-Laythi (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The promise is a gift.""**"
"6835","Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman (رحمه الله) said: I entered upon Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) and he asked me, thinking I belonged to Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, so I said: I am only of Kalbiyyah. Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon me in my house and said: I said: I am stronger than that. He said: I said: I am stronger than that. He said: I said: I do fast it, O Messenger of Allah. He said: I said: I am stronger than that. He said: I said: I am stronger than that. He said: > **""Have I not been informed that you recite the Quran every day and night? Recite it in every month.""** > **""Then recite it in every half month.""** > **""Then recite it in every seven, and do not increase. And it has reached me that you fast perpetually.""** > **""Then fast three days of every month.""** > **""Then fast two days of every week.""** > **""Then fast the fast of Dawud, a day and break a day, for it is the most just fasting with Allah. And he would not break when he promised, nor flee when he met.""**"
"6836","Hudhayfah (رضي الله عنه) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever stipulates for his brother a condition he does not intend to fulfil for him is like one who lets his neighbour down to no protection.""**"
"6837","Al-Jarud (رضي الله عنه) said: I said — or a man said — O Messenger of Allah, a find we find? He said: > **""Announce it, do not conceal nor hide. If you find its owner, then hand it to him; otherwise it is the property of Allah which He gives to whom He wills.""**"
"6838","Al-Jarud (رضي الله عنه) also said in a narration: While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on one of his journeys, with few mounts, the people recalled mounts. So I said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: You know what we suffer of mounts. He said: I said: Camels we shall come upon at Jurf, so we may benefit by their backs. He said: Then he mentioned the hadith. > **""And what suffices us?""** > **""No; the stray of the Muslim is a brand of Fire, let him not approach it. The stray of the Muslim is a brand of Fire, let him not approach it.""**"
"6839","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the find, and he said: > **""It is announced, not hidden nor concealed. If its owner comes, well, otherwise it is the property of Allah which He gives to whom He wills.""**"
"6840","Ismah (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, three times: > **""The stray of the Muslim is a brand of Fire.""**"
"6841","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the stray sheep and said: And he was asked about the stray camel and said: > **""It is for you or for your brother or for the wolf.""** > **""What have you to do with it? With it are its water-vessel and its shoes. Leave it until its owner finds it.""**"
"6842","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The find is not lawful. Whoever picks up anything, let him announce it. If its owner comes, let him return it to him. If he does not come, let him give it in charity. If he then comes, let him give him choice between the reward and what belongs to him.""**"
"6843","Abu Wail Shaqiq ibn Salamah (رحمه الله) said: Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) bought a slave-girl from a man for six hundred or seven hundred dirhams, then sought him for a year and did not find him. Then he went out with her to hardship and gave her in charity, dirham by dirham, on behalf of her owner. If her owner comes he will give him choice: if he chooses the reward it is his, and if he chooses his property it is his. Then Ibn Masud said: Do thus with the find."
"6844","Uqbah ibn Suwayd, from his father (رضي الله عنه), said: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the sheep. He said: And I asked him about the camel — and when he grew angry it was known by redness of his cheeks — he said: And I asked him about the find, and he said: > **""For you or for your brother or for the wolf.""** > **""What have you to do with it? With it are its water-vessel and its shoes; it comes to water and goes to herbage. Leave its way until its owner meets it.""** > **""Announce it, then bind its knot and its bag. If its owner comes, hand it to him; otherwise it is yours.""**"
"6845","Abu Thalabah (رضي الله عنه) said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him, and he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, a sprout of good or a sprout of evil? He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I went out with an uncle of mine on a journey and he suffered barefootedness. He said: Lend me your shoes. I said: I shall lend them on condition you marry me to your daughter. He said: I have married her to you. When we came to his family he sent me my shoes and said: You have no wife with us. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, I vowed to slaughter camels of mine upon an idol of mine from the idols of Jahiliyyah. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, silver found near an inhabited village or a frequented road? He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, a sheep we find in desert land? He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, a she-camel or camel found in desert land with vessel and water-skin upon it? He said: And he mentioned the hadith, some of it in the Sunan. > **""A small sprout.""** > **""No, rather a sprout of good.""** > **""There is no good for you in her.""** > **""Fulfil your vow, and do not sin against your Lord.""** > **""There is no fulfilment of a vow in disobedience, nor severing of kin, nor in what one does not own.""** > **""Announce it a year. If its owner comes, hand it to him; otherwise count its knot, its vessel and its number, then enjoy it.""** > **""Eat it, for it is only for you or for your brother or for the wolf.""** > **""Leave it alone; what have you to do with it.""**"
"6846","Ali ibn Abi Talib (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no writing cast in a waste place of the earth except Allah sends to it angels sheltering it with their wings and sanctifying it, until Allah sends to it a wali from His awliya who lifts it from the earth. Whoever lifts a writing wherein is a Name from the Names of Allah, Allah lifts his name in Illiyyun and lightens the punishment from his parents, even if they are disbelievers.""**"
"6847","Yala ibn Murrah (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever picks up a small find, a dirham or a rope or the like, let him announce it three days. If more than that, let him announce it six days.""**"
"6848","Yala ibn Murrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""Whoever picks up a small find, a garment or the like, let him announce it three days. Whoever picks up more than that, six days. If its owner comes, well, otherwise let him give it in charity. If its owner comes, let him give him choice.""**"
"6849","Abu Said al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) reported that Ali ibn Abi Talib (رضي الله عنه) found a dinar in the market and came to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: So he announced it three days and found none who knew it. He returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him, and he said: So Ali sold it and bought with it barley for three dirhams and dates for three dirhams, settled three dirhams of debt, bought meat for a dirham and oil for a dirham; and the dinar was eleven dirhams. After that its owner came and knew it, so Ali said to him: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded me. The owner of the dinar went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned that to him, so he said to Ali: He said: I have consumed it. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the man: > **""Announce it three days.""** > **""It is yours.""** > **""Return it.""** > **""When anything comes to us, we shall pay you.""**"
"6850","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (رضي الله عنه) said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he found two dates; he took one date and gave me the other. And in the wording of Abu Yala: I was walking with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he found a cluster-stalk wherein were two dates; he took a date and gave me a date."
"6851","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed I find a date fallen and take it and eat it.""**"
"6852","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man announcing a stray in the mosque and said: > **""May you not find.""**"
"6853","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) said: A man entered announcing a stray in the mosque, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""May you not find.""**"
"6854","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه), said: We were commanded, when we see one announcing a stray in the mosque, to say to him: May you not find."
"6855","Abu Jamilah (رحمه الله) reported that he found a foundling in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) and brought him to him. He suspected him, then he was praised with good, so Umar said: He is free, his wala is yours, and his maintenance is upon us from the Bayt al-Mal."
"6856","Al-Zuhri (رحمه الله) reported in a narration that a man informed him that he picked up a child of zina."
"6857","Al-Zuhri (رحمه الله) reported in a narration that a man came to his family having picked up a foundling, then went to Umar (رضي الله عنه) and mentioned it to him. So Umar said: Perhaps the little cave is a calamity. The man said: It was only picked up while I was absent. So Umar asked about him and he was praised. Umar said to him: His wala is yours, and his maintenance is upon us from the Bayt al-Mal."
"6858","Abu Amr al-Shaybani (رحمه الله) said: I came to Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) with runaways from the slaves of Yemen, and he said: Reward and booty. I said: As for reward, we know it; what is booty? He said: Forty dirhams for every head."
"6859","Abu Humayd al-Saidi (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is not lawful for a Muslim to take the property of his brother without right, and that is because Allah has made the property of the Muslim forbidden to the Muslim.""**"
"6860","And in a narration: > **""It is not lawful for a Muslim to take the staff of his brother without goodwill.""**"
"6861","And in a narration: > **""It is not lawful for a Muslim to take the staff.""**"
"6862","Amr ibn Yathribi (رضي الله عنه) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us and said: So I said: O Messenger of Allah, what if I see the sheep of my cousin, shall I take a sheep thereof? He said: > **""Lo, nothing of the property of one's brother is lawful except with goodwill from him.""** > **""If you met it as a ewe carrying a knife and flint in the desolate land of al-Jamish — a land between Makkah and al-Jar wherein is no companion — then do not disturb it.""**"
"6863","Amr ibn Yathribi (رضي الله عنه) said in a narration: I heard the sermon of the Prophet ﷺ at Mina, and among what he addressed was that he said: When I heard that I said: O Messenger of Allah, what if I meet the sheep of my cousin — and he mentioned the like. > **""Nothing of the property of one's brother is lawful except what his soul is generous with.""**"
"6864","Wathilah ibn al-Asqa (رضي الله عنه) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: — and he gestured with his hand to the heart. > **""The Muslim is forbidden to the Muslim: his blood, his honour and his property. The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he does not wrong him nor forsake him. Taqwa is here""**"
"6865","Abdullah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""The sanctity of the property of the Muslim is like the sanctity of his blood.""**"
"6866","Abu Hurrah al-Raqashi, from his uncle (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The property of a Muslim man is not lawful except with goodwill from him.""**"
"6867","Talib ibn Salma ibn Asim ibn al-Hakam (رحمه الله) said: One of my family related to me that my grandfather related to them that he witnessed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in a sermon, saying: > **""Lo, your properties and your bloods are forbidden to you as the sanctity of this land on this day. Lo, let me not find you returning after me disbelievers striking the necks of one another. Lo, let the present of you convey to the absent. I do not know whether I shall ever meet you after this day. O Allah, bear witness over them. O Allah, have I conveyed.""**"
"6868","Al-Saib ibn Yazid (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Let none of you take the belongings of his companion in play nor in earnest. When one of you has taken the belongings of his companion, let him return it to him.""**"
"6869","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions passed by a woman who had slaughtered for them a sheep and prepared food for them. When he returned she said: O Messenger of Allah, we slaughtered for you a sheep and prepared food for you, so enter and eat. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions entered, and they would not begin until the Prophet ﷺ began. The Prophet ﷺ took a morsel but could not swallow it. So the Prophet ﷺ said: The woman said: O Messenger of Allah, we are not formal with the family of Muadh; we take from them and they take from us. > **""This sheep was slaughtered without permission of its owners.""**"
"6870","Abu Musa (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a people of the Ansar in their abode, and they slaughtered for him a sheep and made food for him from it. He took some meat to eat it and chewed it a while without swallowing. He said: They said: It is the sheep of so-and-so; we slaughtered it until its owner comes that we may please him with its price. He said: > **""What is the affair of this meat?""** > **""Give it to the captives.""**"
"6871","Rafi ibn Khadij (رضي الله عنه) said: One day I entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and with them was a pot boiling with meat. A piece of fat pleased me, so I took it and swallowed it lightly, and I complained thereof for a year. Then I mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: Then he wiped my belly and I cast it up green. By Him Who sent him with the truth, I have not complained of my belly until this hour. > **""Indeed therein was the breath of seven persons.""**"
"6872","Dhuayb (رضي الله عنه) reported that the delegation of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by Umm Zubayb and took her carpet. So Zubayb rode to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, the delegation took my mother's carpet. So the Prophet ﷺ said: Then he who took his mother's carpet took from him a sa of barley, his sword and his belt in exchange. Then the Prophet ﷺ raised his hand and wiped with it the head of Zubayb, then said: > **""Return to him his mother's carpet.""** > **""May Allah bless you, O boy, and bless your mother through you.""**"
"6873","Jabir ibn Samurah (رضي الله عنه) said: The enemy seized a she-camel of a man of Banu Sulaym, then a Muslim bought it from them. Its owner recognized it and came to the Prophet ﷺ, so the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to take it for the price for which he bought it from the enemy, otherwise leave it between him and it."
"6874","Abu Lubabah al-Aslami (رضي الله عنه) reported that a she-camel of his herd was stolen, and he found it with a man of the Ansar. He said to him: My she-camel, and I establish proof upon it. So he established proof upon it before the Prophet ﷺ, while the Ansari established that he bought it for eighteen from a polytheist of Taif. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled, then said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have not with me what to give him today, but dates will come to me at harvest. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What you wish, O Abu Lubabah. If you wish, pay him eighteen and take the mount, and if you wish, leave it.""** > **""That is to him.""**"
"6875","Umar (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""Whoever finds his property in the fay before it is divided is more entitled to it. Whoever finds it after it is divided has nothing.""**"
"6876","Yazid ibn Abi Habib (رحمه الله) reported that Abu al-Darda (رضي الله عنه) came to two men disputing in Egypt concerning land. So Abu al-Darda said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: So Abu al-Darda thereupon left for Sham. > **""When you see two Muslim brothers disputing over a handspan of land, then leave that land.""**"
"6877","Ubadah (رضي الله عنه) said: Indeed among the decree of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is: > **""There is no right for an unjust vein.""**"
"6878","Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which wrong is most wrongful? He said: > **""A cubit of land which a Muslim man diminishes from the right of his brother, except he will be collared with it on the Day of Resurrection to the bottom of the earth, whose bottom none knows but Allah Exalted Who created it.""**"
"6879","Abu Malik al-Ashari (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""The greatest misappropriation with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is a cubit of land. You find two men neighbours in land or in a house, and one of them cuts off a cubit from the share of his companion. When he cuts it off, he will be collared with it from seven earths until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6880","Abu Malik al-Ashjai (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ the like of it."
"6881","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""Whoever takes a handspan of land without his right will be collared with it from seven earths.""**"
"6882","Yala ibn Murrah (رضي الله عنه) said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Whatever man wrongs a handspan of land, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, charges him to dig it until he reaches the last of seven earths, then He collars him with it until the Day of Resurrection, until judgment is passed among people.""**"
"6883","Yala ibn Murrah al-Thaqafi (رضي الله عنه) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever takes land without its right will be charged to carry its dust to the Gathering.""**"
"6884","Yala (رضي الله عنه) reported with al-Tabarani also: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever wrongs a handspan of land or more will be charged to dig it until he reaches water, then carry it to the Gathering.""**"
"6885","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, warning against taking anything from the earth without lawful right. > **""Whoever takes anything from the earth without its lawful due will be collared with it from seven earths; neither sarf nor 'adl will be accepted from him.""**"
"6886","Abdullah ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, concerning taking land in Makkah and elsewhere without right. > **""Whoever takes a span from Makkah, it is as if he took it from beneath the Foot of the Most Merciful; and whoever takes anything from the rest of the earth without its right will come on the Day of Resurrection with his neck collared from seven earths.""**"
"6887","Al-Hakam ibn al-Harith al-Sulami (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, concerning taking from the road of the Muslims. > **""Whoever takes a span from the road of the Muslims will come carrying it from seven earths.""**"
"6888","Shaddad ibn Aws (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, concerning usurping land and being killed defending one's wealth. > **""Whoever takes a span of land will be collared with it from seven earths. And whoever is killed defending his wealth is a martyr.""**"
"6889","Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, concerning wrongfully taking land. > **""Whoever takes a span of land wrongfully will be collared with it on the Day of Resurrection from seven earths.""**"
"6890","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (رضي الله عنه) said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say, concerning taking land without right. > **""Whoever takes a span of land will be collared with it on the Day of Resurrection from seven earths.""**"
"6891","Abdullah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, concerning usurping another man's land wrongfully. > **""Whoever usurps a man's land wrongfully will meet Allah while He is angry with him.""**"
"6892","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, concerning wrongfully taking land. > **""Whoever wrongs a span of land will come on the Day of Resurrection collared with it from seven earths upon his neck.""**"
"6893","Abdullah ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, concerning the gravest lies and changing the boundaries of land, while part of it concerning the false dream is established in the Sahih. > **""The gravest of lies: one who claims himself for other than his father, and the gravest of lies: one who shows his two eyes in sleep what they did not see, and one who changes the landmarks of the earth.""**"
"6894","Amr ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, concerning changing the landmarks of the earth. > **""Whoever changes the landmarks of the earth, upon him is the curse of Allah and His anger on the Day of Resurrection; Allah will accept from him neither sarf nor 'adl.""**"
